window.LING285_BYTOPIC = {"topics": [{"id": "mass-spring", "title": "Masses, springs and frequency", "lectures": "Lectures 11 and 12", "blocks": [{"ref": "Lecture 11 · p2 · Lecture 12 · p3", "main": "**A mass-spring system is a mass attached to a spring, and it vibrates because the spring and the mass want different things.** The spring has a resting length, called its **equilibrium position**. The spring always pulls or pushes toward that position. A moving mass keeps moving until something stops it, so it does not stop by itself.", "sub": ["If the spring is stretched, it pulls back. If the spring is compressed, it pushes back.", "The spring \"wants\" to rest, and the moving mass \"wants\" to keep going, so neither one ever wins."], "end": "So the mass keeps going past the middle, and the spring keeps turning it around.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture11/002.webp", "video": "assets/original/3a173c997457e2f0c60b.mp4", "box": [0.0, 0.0, 100.0, 100.0], "poster": "assets/exam2/frames/3a173c997457e2f0c60b.jpg", "caption": "Lecture 11 · p2 · with the video played on the slide"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 11 · p2", "main": "**The vibration of a mass-spring system is a cycle of four events that repeats.** First, the mass is pulled out and let go, so the stretched spring pulls it back toward the middle. Second, the mass reaches the middle while still moving, so it keeps going and compresses the spring.", "sub": ["Third, the compressed spring pushes the mass back the other way.", "Fourth, the mass passes the middle again and stretches the spring, so the cycle starts over. This repeating back and forth is called **vibration**.", "**Friction is left out of the model.** On a real table, friction slowly stops the motion. In the model there is no friction, so the mass vibrates forever."], "end": "", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture11/002.webp", "video": "assets/original/3a173c997457e2f0c60b.mp4", "box": [0.0, 0.0, 100.0, 100.0], "poster": "assets/exam2/frames/3a173c997457e2f0c60b.jpg", "caption": "Lecture 11 · p2 · with the video played on the slide"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 11 · p2 · Lecture 12 · p3", "main": "**The same mass-spring system can vibrate in two directions, and both directions follow exactly the same rules.** In **longitudinal** motion, the mass moves left and right along the line of the spring. In **transverse** motion, the mass sits between two walls with one spring on each side. It moves up and down, across the line of the springs.", "sub": ["In transverse motion, pulling the mass up stretches both springs, so the stretched springs pull the mass back down.", "When the mass passes the middle, the springs are not stretched. The mass is still moving, so it keeps going and stretches the springs on the other side.", "Both versions vibrate at the **same rate**, because they have the same mass and the same total stiffness in the direction the mass moves.", "Some systems are easier to understand in one direction than the other. The vocal folds and the air in the vocal tract each vibrate most clearly in a different direction."], "end": "That is why both directions matter for speech.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture11/002.webp", "video": "assets/original/3a173c997457e2f0c60b.mp4", "box": [0.0, 0.0, 100.0, 100.0], "poster": "assets/exam2/frames/3a173c997457e2f0c60b.jpg", "caption": "Lecture 11 · p2 · with the video played on the slide"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 11 · p3 · Lecture 12 · p4", "main": "**How fast a mass-spring system vibrates depends on two things, its mass and its stiffness.** **Frequency** is the number of back-and-forth cycles a system completes in one second. **Raising the mass lowers the frequency, and raising the stiffness raises the frequency.** Both rules work the same way for longitudinal and transverse motion.", "sub": ["**A heavier mass vibrates more slowly**, because a heavy mass is hard to start moving and hard to turn around. So each trip back and forth takes longer. Heavy tree branches sway slowly, and small branches sway quickly.", "Making the mass **four times heavier cuts the rate in half**.", "**A stiffer spring makes the system vibrate faster**, because it pulls back harder and turns the mass around sooner. Making the spring **four times stiffer doubles the rate**."], "end": "That is why mass and stiffness push the frequency in opposite directions.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture11/003.webp", "video": "assets/original/e7a1853082e98dd26757.mp4", "box": [0.0, 0.0, 100.0, 100.0], "poster": "assets/exam2/frames/e7a1853082e98dd26757.jpg", "caption": "Lecture 11 · p3 · with the video played on the slide"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 11 · p3", "main": "**A mass changes the frequency only while it moves, and a spring changes the frequency only while it stretches or compresses.** A mass that never moves could be made a thousand pounds heavier. The frequency would not change at all.", "sub": ["A spring that never stretches or compresses adds no stiffness, so it cannot raise the frequency."], "end": "So the mass and stiffness rules apply only where each part is doing its job.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture11/003.webp", "video": "assets/original/e7a1853082e98dd26757.mp4", "box": [0.0, 0.0, 100.0, 100.0], "poster": "assets/exam2/frames/e7a1853082e98dd26757.jpg", "caption": "Lecture 11 · p3 · with the video played on the slide"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 12 · p2", "main": "**The same math that describes a vibrating spring also describes the ups and downs of an economy.** The **2026 Nobel Prize in economics** went to **Robert Hall** and **Michael Woodford**. The prize was for the **microeconomic foundations of macroeconomic stabilization**.", "sub": ["**Stabilization** means bringing a system back to its equilibrium and keeping it there.", "Stabilization is the same job that **balancing feedback** does. It is also the job a spring does when it pulls a mass back to rest."], "end": "", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture12/002.webp", "caption": "Lecture 12 · p2"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 12 · p2", "main": "**In the Hall and Woodford model, unemployment and inflation rise and fall in turns, like the two curves of an oscillator.** One curve acts like the position of a mass. It starts high, goes negative and comes back. The other curve acts like the velocity, which is the flipped version of the first curve.", "sub": ["The goal of stabilization policy is to **make these ups and downs smaller**.", "Smaller ups and downs let the economy avoid high unemployment and runaway inflation."], "end": "", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture12/002.webp", "caption": "Lecture 12 · p2"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 12 · p2", "main": "**Woodford's theory is called Dynamic Stochastic General Equilibrium, or DSGE, and each word has a meaning.** **Dynamic** means the economy changes over time by following rules. **Equilibrium** means the goal is a steady state without wild jumps.", "sub": ["**Stochastic** means the model can handle **random shocks**, such as a war starting or a company leader being caught stealing money.", "Stochastic gradient descent works the same way. One strange data point can push the estimate off, and later data pull it back toward the average.", "**Macroeconomic** quantities describe a whole economy, such as inflation or the number of people without jobs. **Microeconomic** events are single choices, such as one person buying something.", "**Large patterns can come from many tiny events.** In speech, many tiny vibrations in the mouth add up to a meaning that a listener understands without tracking each vibration."], "end": "So speech and the economy share the same structure, with big patterns built from many small events.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture12/002.webp", "caption": "Lecture 12 · p2"}}], "questions": [{"source": "Review 5", "q": "What do you get when you have an infinite number of masses and springs?", "a": "An infinite number of masses and springs gives a string, such as a guitar or piano string. Every small piece of a string has mass, and every small piece stretches the way a spring does. So a string vibrates in modes the same way a chain of masses does, only with infinitely many of them. When the masses and springs are spread over a surface instead of a line, the result is a membrane, such as a drum or the vocal folds."}, {"source": "Predicted", "q": "What happens to the frequency of a single mass on a spring if you make the spring stiffer? If you make the mass heavier?", "a": "A stiffer spring raises the frequency, and a heavier mass lowers it. A stiffer spring pulls the mass back toward equilibrium with more force, so the mass turns around sooner. A heavier mass is harder to start and stop, so each back-and-forth cycle takes longer and the frequency goes down."}, {"source": "", "q": "How can longitudinal and transverse vibration work the same way?", "a": "**Both directions combine a restoring force with inertia.** The restoring force pulls the mass back toward equilibrium. Because of inertia, the mass keeps moving past equilibrium, so it overshoots. Changing the direction of the motion does not change that interaction."}, {"source": "", "q": "What changes between the two directions, and what stays the same?", "a": "**The direction of the motion changes, but the restoring force and the overshoot stay the same.** Longitudinal motion goes along the system, and transverse motion goes across it. In both, the system pulls the mass back and inertia carries it past equilibrium."}, {"source": "", "q": "Why does more mass lower the frequency while more stiffness raises it?", "a": "**More mass slows the system down, and more stiffness speeds it up.** More mass has more inertia, so it is harder to turn around and each cycle takes longer. A stiffer spring pulls back harder, so the mass returns sooner and the frequency goes up."}, {"source": "", "q": "Why do you have to say what stayed the same when you predict a frequency change?", "a": "**Because mass and stiffness push the frequency in opposite directions.** If both change at once, their effects compete. So you have to know what stayed the same before you can use one rule."}, {"source": "", "q": "How can an economic model and a spring share the same kind of feedback?", "a": "**Both describe a move away from a stable level and rules that change it over time.** In both, feedback can pull the system back or push it further away. What they share is that feedback, not their material."}, {"source": "", "q": "What has to match for two very different systems to be compared?", "a": "**The causes and effects behind the prediction have to match.** Two systems can look alike on the surface and still work differently. A mechanism only carries over when the cause-to-effect links are the same."}]}, {"id": "modes", "title": "Modes: how a system with many masses vibrates", "lectures": "Lectures 11, 12 and 13", "blocks": [{"ref": "Lecture 11 · p4 · Lecture 12 · p5", "main": "**A system of two masses joined by springs can vibrate in two different ways, and each way is called a mode.** The system has two walls, one spring from each wall to a mass, and **one spring between the two masses**. All the masses are equal, and all the springs have the same stiffness. A **mode** is a way of vibrating.", "sub": ["The two modes are told apart by one question, which is whether the masses move together or against each other.", "In **Mode 1**, the masses move together. Both masses are pulled up and let go, so they rise and fall side by side. In longitudinal motion, both are pulled left, so they move left and right as a pair.", "In **Mode 2**, the masses move against each other. One mass is pulled down and the other up, so one rises whenever the other falls. In longitudinal motion, the masses move toward each other and then apart."], "end": "", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture11/004.webp", "video": "assets/original/57ff4733438cce3597cb.mp4", "box": [0.0, 0.0, 100.0, 100.0], "poster": "assets/exam2/frames/57ff4733438cce3597cb.jpg", "caption": "Lecture 11 · p4 · with the video played on the slide"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 11 · p4 · Lecture 12 · p5", "main": "**Two masses have two modes, and Mode 2 is faster because more springs are stretching.** Both modes use the same two masses and the same three springs. So the difference comes from what each spring does. In both modes, the **two outer springs** stretch and compress.", "sub": ["In **Mode 1**, the masses move together, so the **middle spring never stretches or compresses**. It moves with the masses as a solid bar, so **Mode 1 effectively has only two springs**.", "In **Mode 2**, the masses move against each other, so the middle spring is stretched and compressed again and again. **All three springs are working**, so Mode 2 has three units of stiffness instead of two.", "More working stiffness gives a stronger pull back to the middle, and a stronger pull gives **a higher frequency**."], "end": "So the springs themselves did not change, and only the way each mode uses them changed.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture11/004.webp", "video": "assets/original/57ff4733438cce3597cb.mp4", "box": [0.0, 0.0, 100.0, 100.0], "poster": "assets/exam2/frames/57ff4733438cce3597cb.jpg", "caption": "Lecture 11 · p4 · with the video played on the slide"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 11 · p4 · Lecture 13 · p3", "main": "**The more the masses move against each other, the more the springs stretch, so the higher the frequency.** This rule holds for every system of masses and springs. **A higher mode does not have stiffer springs.** A higher mode makes the same springs do more stretching.", "sub": ["More parts moving against each other means more springs are stretching. So **higher modes are stiffer and faster**."], "end": "That is why a system with several masses has several frequencies, each one higher than the last.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture11/004.webp", "video": "assets/original/57ff4733438cce3597cb.mp4", "box": [0.0, 0.0, 100.0, 100.0], "poster": "assets/exam2/frames/57ff4733438cce3597cb.jpg", "caption": "Lecture 11 · p4 · with the video played on the slide"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 12 · p5", "main": "**A spring only matters in a mode where that spring stretches.** Suppose the **middle spring is made stiffer** while the two-mass system vibrates.", "sub": ["**Mode 1 does not change**, because the middle spring never stretches in Mode 1.", "**Mode 2 gets faster**, because the middle spring stretches and compresses all the time in Mode 2."], "end": "So one change to a spring can raise one mode and leave another mode alone.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture12/005.webp", "video": "assets/original/57ff4733438cce3597cb.mp4", "box": [0.0, 0.0, 100.0, 100.0], "poster": "assets/exam2/frames/57ff4733438cce3597cb.jpg", "caption": "Lecture 12 · p5 · with the video played on the slide"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 11 · p4 · Lecture 12 · p5", "main": "**A system can also vibrate in both of its modes at the same time.** To start this mixed motion, both masses are pulled up, but one mass is pulled higher than the other. Real movement and real speech production do this all the time.", "sub": ["Both masses fall together, which is Mode 1.", "The masses start at different heights, so the middle spring also pushes them against each other, which is Mode 2.", "So the masses move together and against each other at once."], "end": "", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture11/004.webp", "video": "assets/original/57ff4733438cce3597cb.mp4", "box": [0.0, 0.0, 100.0, 100.0], "poster": "assets/exam2/frames/57ff4733438cce3597cb.jpg", "caption": "Lecture 11 · p4 · with the video played on the slide"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 11 · p6 · Lecture 12 · p6", "main": "**Three masses joined by four springs have three modes, and each mode has its own frequency from lowest to highest.** In **Mode 1**, all three masses move together. In **Mode 2**, the middle mass stays still while the two outer masses move against each other. In **Mode 3**, the middle mass moves opposite to the two outer masses.", "sub": ["**Mode 1 has the lowest frequency**, because the masses move together. So the two middle springs do not stretch, and springs that do not stretch add no stiffness.", "**Mode 2 has the middle frequency**, because the springs stretch and compress, but less than they could. To start Mode 2, the outer masses are pulled apart without touching the middle mass.", "**Mode 3 has the highest frequency**, because every mass moves against its neighbor. So the springs stretch the most. In transverse motion, the middle mass goes down while both outer masses go up."], "end": "", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture11/006.webp", "video": "assets/original/6fdaa70e0bfab83a6d83.mp4", "box": [0.0, 0.0, 100.0, 100.0], "poster": "assets/exam2/frames/6fdaa70e0bfab83a6d83.jpg", "caption": "Lecture 11 · p6 · with the video played on the slide"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 11 · p6 · Lecture 12 · p6", "main": "**Changing one mass only affects the modes where that mass moves.** The middle mass is called **Mass 2**. Suppose Mass 2 is made heavier.", "sub": ["**Mode 1 gets slower**, because Mass 2 moves in Mode 1.", "**Mode 3 gets slower**, because Mass 2 moves in Mode 3.", "**Mode 2 does not change**, because Mass 2 does not move in Mode 2.", "**The amount a mass changes the frequency depends on how much that mass moves.** A mass that does not move has no effect. A mass that moves a little has a small effect, and a mass that moves a lot has a large effect."], "end": "So a mass changes frequency only by moving.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture11/006.webp", "video": "assets/original/6fdaa70e0bfab83a6d83.mp4", "box": [0.0, 0.0, 100.0, 100.0], "poster": "assets/exam2/frames/6fdaa70e0bfab83a6d83.jpg", "caption": "Lecture 11 · p6 · with the video played on the slide"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 11 · p6", "main": "**All three modes can happen at the same time, so any motion of the system is a mix of its pure modes.** However the system is started, it vibrates as some mix of these **pure modes**.", "sub": ["Learning a musical instrument means learning to make its masses and springs vibrate in some modes and not others.", "How a string is plucked or a violin is bowed decides which modes start. Those modes are heard as **notes**.", "A **trained singer** does the same thing with the vocal folds. The singer controls their mass and stiffness to sing in tune."], "end": "", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture11/006.webp", "video": "assets/original/6fdaa70e0bfab83a6d83.mp4", "box": [0.0, 0.0, 100.0, 100.0], "poster": "assets/exam2/frames/6fdaa70e0bfab83a6d83.jpg", "caption": "Lecture 11 · p6 · with the video played on the slide"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 11 · p7 · Lecture 12 · p7 · Lecture 13 · p3", "main": "**Every added mass adds one more mode, so N masses give N modes.** One mass has one mode, two masses have two modes, and six masses have six modes. Higher modes have more **reversals**. A reversal is a place where two neighboring masses move in opposite directions.", "sub": ["More reversals mean more stretching, so **higher modes have higher frequencies**.", "In a chain of separate masses, the mode frequencies are usually **not whole-number multiples** of the lowest frequency."], "end": "", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture11/007.webp", "video": "assets/original/a6ce5f4f318fd7141922.mp4", "box": [0.0, 0.0, 100.0, 100.0], "poster": "assets/exam2/frames/a6ce5f4f318fd7141922.jpg", "caption": "Lecture 11 · p7 · with the video played on the slide"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 11 · p7 · Lecture 12 · p7 · Lecture 13 · p3", "main": "**The same mode has the same pattern in every chain, no matter how many masses the chain has.** **Mode 1** always has everything moving up and down together. **Mode 2** always splits the system into **two parts**, so the left side moves against the right side. **Mode 3** always splits the system into **three parts**, so the middle moves against the two sides.", "sub": ["With an odd number of masses, such as three, five or seven, the middle mass stays still in Mode 2.", "With **four masses**, Mode 1 moves all four together, and Mode 2 moves the left pair against the right pair. Mode 3 moves the middle pair against the outer pair, and Mode 4 moves every mass against its neighbors."], "end": "", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture11/007.webp", "video": "assets/original/a6ce5f4f318fd7141922.mp4", "box": [0.0, 0.0, 100.0, 100.0], "poster": "assets/exam2/frames/a6ce5f4f318fd7141922.jpg", "caption": "Lecture 11 · p7 · with the video played on the slide"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 11 · p7 · Lecture 12 · p7", "main": "**A chain with an infinite number of masses and springs is a string**, such as a guitar string or a piano string. Every small piece of a string has weight, which is its **mass**. Every small piece resists being stretched or compressed, which is its **springiness**. So a string has an infinite number of modes.", "sub": ["The string has the same mode patterns. Everything moves together in Mode 1, and the two halves move against each other in Mode 2. In Mode 3, the middle moves against the sides.", "In the **lowest mode**, the whole string moves together with almost no stretching, so this mode gives the **lowest note**. In each higher mode, more parts of the string move against each other.", "A string plucked without skill vibrates in many modes at once.", "The frequencies of a string are heard as **notes**. The ear can pick out the notes of many instruments at once, because the ear is also built from masses and springs."], "end": "So the ear responds to the masses and springs vibrating in the world because it is made of masses and springs too.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture11/007.webp", "video": "assets/original/a6ce5f4f318fd7141922.mp4", "box": [0.0, 0.0, 100.0, 100.0], "poster": "assets/exam2/frames/a6ce5f4f318fd7141922.jpg", "caption": "Lecture 11 · p7 · with the video played on the slide"}}], "questions": [{"source": "Review 1", "q": "Why do multiple mass systems have multiple frequencies?", "a": "Multiple mass systems have multiple frequencies because the masses can move in several different ways, called modes. In some modes the masses move together, and in others they move against each other. When the masses move against each other, the springs between them are stretched and compressed more, so more stiffness is working and that mode vibrates faster. So each mode has its own frequency, and a system with N masses has N modes and N frequencies."}, {"source": "Review 2", "q": "How many modes does a 3-mass system have?", "a": "A 3-mass system has three modes. It has three because a system with N masses has N modes of vibration."}, {"source": "Lab 9", "q": "Four masses are joined by springs between two walls. How many modes does the system have, and what are the masses doing in the lowest and highest modes?", "a": "The system has four modes, because there is one mode for each mass. In the lowest mode, Mode 1, all four masses move in the same direction at the same time. In the highest mode, Mode 4, every mass moves opposite to its neighbors. So every spring is stretched and compressed, and the frequency is highest."}, {"source": "Review 3", "q": "Which mode of the 3-mass system has the highest frequency? Why?", "a": "Mode 3 has the highest frequency. In Mode 3 the middle mass moves opposite to the two outer masses, so every spring is stretched and compressed as much as possible. That puts the most stiffness to work, and more stiffness makes the system vibrate faster."}, {"source": "Predicted", "q": "Which mode of a 2-mass system has the lowest frequency, and why?", "a": "Mode 1 has the lowest frequency. In Mode 1 the two masses move together, so the middle spring is carried along without stretching. Only the two outer springs are working, so Mode 1 has less stiffness than Mode 2 and vibrates more slowly."}, {"source": "Review 4", "q": "If you increase mass 2, what happens to the frequencies of each of the modes?", "a": "If mass 2 increases, the frequencies of Mode 1 and Mode 3 go down, and Mode 2 stays the same. The middle mass moves in Mode 1 and in Mode 3, so making it heavier slows those modes and lowers their frequencies. In Mode 2 the middle mass does not move, and a mass that does not move has no effect on the frequency."}, {"source": "Predicted", "q": "In the 3-mass system, what happens to each mode if you make mass 1 (an outer mass) heavier?", "a": "All three modes go down when mass 1 is made heavier. The outer mass moves in Mode 1, Mode 2 and Mode 3, and a heavier mass that is moving lowers the frequency. No mode leaves an outer mass still, so none of the three frequencies stays the same."}, {"source": "Predicted", "q": "In a 2-mass system, what happens to each mode if you make the middle spring stiffer?", "a": "Mode 1 stays the same and Mode 2 goes up. In Mode 1 the masses move together, so the middle spring is never stretched and its extra stiffness does nothing. In Mode 2 the masses move against each other, so the middle spring is stretched and compressed, and the extra stiffness raises the frequency."}, {"source": "", "q": "How are the two normal modes of a two-mass system different?", "a": "**In Mode 1 the two masses move together, and in Mode 2 they move in opposite directions.** Each mode is a pattern of the whole system, so both masses take part in both modes. Mode 2 has the higher frequency."}, {"source": "", "q": "Why does Mode 2 have a higher frequency than Mode 1?", "a": "**Mode 2 is faster because more springs are stretching.** When the masses move in opposite directions, the middle spring stretches a lot. That adds restoring force, so the masses turn around sooner and the frequency goes up."}, {"source": "", "q": "What makes the three normal modes of a three-mass system different?", "a": "**Each mode is a different pattern with its own frequency.** In Mode 1 all three masses move together. In Mode 2 the middle mass stays still while the outer masses move in opposite directions. In Mode 3 each mass moves opposite to its neighbor, so it has the highest frequency."}, {"source": "", "q": "Why does adding mass only to the middle mass leave Mode 2 unchanged?", "a": "**Mode 2 does not change because the middle mass does not move in Mode 2.** Extra mass only matters where a mode is moving. The middle mass is a node in Mode 2, so its extra inertia has no motion to slow down."}, {"source": "", "q": "Why does a system gain a mode every time you add a mass that can move on its own?", "a": "**Each new mass adds one more independent way to move, so it adds one more mode.** In the one-direction model, N masses give N modes. So adding a mass always adds a mode."}, {"source": "", "q": "What happens when the masses and springs become a continuous string?", "a": "**The many separate masses turn into a string with a whole series of modes.** The mass and the springiness are spread all along the string. The string still has modes, but their spacing also depends on its shape and on how its ends are held."}]}, {"id": "membrane-method", "title": "Membranes, complex vibrations and testing the theory", "lectures": "Lectures 11, 12, 13 and 14", "blocks": [{"ref": "Lecture 11 · p9 · Lecture 12 · p12 · Lecture 14 · p2", "main": "**The vocal folds are a membrane, so their modes spread across a surface instead of along a line.** A **membrane** is a vibrating sheet, and the vocal folds are a sheet of muscle. The **larynx** holds the vocal folds. They are **fixed on all sides** and are made of many masses and springs, so the same rules of modes apply.", "sub": ["In **Mode 1**, the whole surface moves up and down together. In **Mode 2**, the left half moves against the right half, so half moves up while the other half moves down.", "In **Mode 3**, the surface splits the other way. A middle line stays still, and the two halves on either side of it move against each other.", "In **Mode 4**, four sections move against each other. Higher modes divide the surface into more and more parts, so the patterns get more complicated.", "Each mode is labeled by two numbers. One counts the bumps along the length, and the other counts the bumps across the width."], "end": "So a membrane has many modes of vibration, like a drum.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture11/009.webp", "video": "assets/original/4378fb47c97c7f4785f9.mp4", "box": [0.0, 0.0, 100.0, 100.0], "poster": "assets/exam2/frames/4378fb47c97c7f4785f9.jpg", "caption": "Lecture 11 · p9 · with the video played on the slide"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 11 · p9 · Lecture 13 · p6 · Lecture 14 · p2", "main": "**The vocal folds vibrate in all their modes at once, and the modes add together into one combined motion.** This combined motion is what actually happens in the throat while a person talks. So a voice has a spectrum full of spikes.", "sub": ["The vocal folds are held fixed along their edges, so they **behave like a string**. A string fixed at both ends has evenly spaced harmonics.", "The same copy-counting works on a membrane. The first mode is one bump across the whole membrane. The second mode fits two bumps, so it has twice the frequency.", "The fourth mode fits four ups and downs, so it has four times the frequency.", "The evenly spaced frequencies attached to these membrane modes are teaching values."], "end": "", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture11/009.webp", "video": "assets/original/4378fb47c97c7f4785f9.mp4", "box": [0.0, 0.0, 100.0, 100.0], "poster": "assets/exam2/frames/4378fb47c97c7f4785f9.jpg", "caption": "Lecture 11 · p9 · with the video played on the slide"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 14 · p2", "main": "**Because the vocal folds are fixed-fixed, their frequencies are harmonics.** **Harmonics** are whole-number multiples of the lowest frequency. The lowest harmonic is called **F0**.", "sub": ["Each person has a different F0, but the other harmonics are always multiples of that person's F0."], "end": "", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture14/002.webp", "video": "assets/original/6508c5d6040ceabb87cd.mp4", "box": [0.0, 0.0, 100.0, 100.0], "poster": "assets/exam2/frames/6508c5d6040ceabb87cd.jpg", "caption": "Lecture 14 · p2 · with the video played on the slide"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 12 · p12", "main": "**Changing the mass or stiffness of the vocal folds changes the voice.** People who smoke heavily can develop **nodules** on their vocal folds. A nodule adds mass at one spot and changes the stiffness nearby.", "sub": ["The added mass and changed stiffness make the folds vibrate differently, so the voice sounds different."], "end": "", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture12/012.webp", "video": "assets/original/4378fb47c97c7f4785f9.mp4", "box": [0.0, 0.0, 100.0, 100.0], "poster": "assets/exam2/frames/4378fb47c97c7f4785f9.jpg", "caption": "Lecture 12 · p12 · with the video played on the slide"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 11 · p10 · Lecture 12 · p13", "main": "**Science works by stating a clear theory, solving it to get a prediction, and then measuring to check the prediction.** Theories are often written as **differential equations**, which describe how quantities change over time.", "sub": ["The spring equations are one example. Solving them predicts that a pulled spring goes back and a pushed spring also goes back.", "The equations for the vocal folds are the same type as the spring equations. There are many more of them, and they are slightly more complicated.", "So the ideas from masses and springs are enough to reason about the vocal folds."], "end": "", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture11/010.webp", "caption": "Lecture 11 · p10"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 11 · p10 · Lecture 12 · p13", "main": "**The theory that the vocal folds are a membrane predicts that every voice has a spectrum with evenly spaced harmonics.** The vocal folds vibrate in many modes at once, so their spectrum should have **evenly spaced peaks**. These evenly spaced peaks are called **harmonics**. The **prediction** is that anyone's voice, when measured, will show them.", "sub": ["The prediction was tested in **Praat**, a program for analyzing speech. Each speaker made a sound with the **mouth wide open**, so no particular vowel was being made.", "In one voice, the lowest spike was near **100 hertz**. The next spikes were close to two, three, four and five times that value, so they were **evenly spaced as predicted**.", "In a second voice, the lowest spike was near **220 hertz**. The next spikes were near two and three times that value, so they were evenly spaced but farther apart.", "In another recorded voice, the lowest harmonic was about **130 hertz**, and the next harmonics were close to two and three times that value."], "end": "So **the prediction was correct**, and a theory built from simple masses and springs predicted real speech precisely.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture11/010.webp", "caption": "Lecture 11 · p10"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 11 · p10 · Lecture 12 · p13", "main": "**Two voices have different harmonics because of the mass rule.** The 220 hertz voice has a **higher pitch**, so that speaker's vocal folds are probably **smaller and thinner**. Thinner folds have less mass, and less mass gives a higher frequency. So every harmonic is higher and farther apart.", "sub": ["Each person's lowest frequency is different, but the harmonics above it are always close to multiples of it.", "Each person's vocal folds give their voice its own spectrum, which is part of how people recognize voices.", "Twins share genetics and have similar vocal folds, so twins can be hard to tell apart on the phone.", "How a person says vowels also matters. Vowels depend on the vibration of the **air** above the vocal folds, not on the folds themselves."], "end": "", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture11/010.webp", "caption": "Lecture 11 · p10"}}], "questions": [{"source": "", "q": "How can several modes be part of one membrane motion?", "a": "**The modes add together into one overall motion.** The membrane can carry several organized patterns at the same time. The motion you see is the sum of all of them, so it looks complicated."}, {"source": "", "q": "What can a measurement at one point and its spectrum show about a mix of membrane modes?", "a": "**The point records the modes that move there, and the spectrum separates their frequencies.** Each mode shows up as its own peak. A point at a node of one mode does not move in that mode, so it misses that mode."}, {"source": "", "q": "How can you test a prediction about voice harmonics with a recording?", "a": "**Record a voice, look at its spectrum, and measure the distances between the peaks.** The membrane theory predicts evenly spaced harmonics. If the peaks are evenly spaced, the recording supports the prediction."}, {"source": "", "q": "How do a theory, a prediction and a measurement fit together?", "a": "**The theory proposes how something works, the prediction says what pattern to expect, and the measurement checks for it.** A real test needs all three linked together. Without that link, a theory is only an idea that sounds plausible."}]}, {"id": "fixed-ends", "title": "Fixed-fixed and fixed-free systems: harmonics and formants", "lectures": "Lectures 11, 12, 13 and 14", "blocks": [{"ref": "Lecture 11 · p8 · Lecture 12 · p8 · Lecture 13 · p5", "main": "**A string fixed at both ends has modes at whole-number multiples of its lowest frequency.** If the lowest mode is at **100 hertz**, the next modes are at **200, 300, 400 and 500 hertz**. **Hertz** means times per second. Modes that line up as whole-number multiples are called **harmonics**.", "sub": ["Each harmonic is a whole number of times the lowest frequency, so the harmonics are evenly spaced."], "end": "", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture11/008.webp", "video": "assets/original/ac3e753564fa139e2612.mp4", "box": [0.0, 0.0, 100.0, 100.0], "poster": "assets/exam2/frames/ac3e753564fa139e2612.jpg", "caption": "Lecture 11 · p8 · with the video played on the slide"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 11 · p8 · Lecture 12 · p8 · Lecture 13 · p4", "main": "**In a string fixed at both ends, each higher mode is made of more copies of the first mode.** The first mode is **one half-wave**. A half-wave is one bump. It goes from no motion at one end to the most motion in the middle, then back to no motion at the other end.", "sub": ["**Mode 2 holds two half-waves**, so one half goes up while the other half goes down. Each half acts as a copy of Mode 1, so Mode 2 has **twice the stiffness** and **twice the frequency**.", "**Mode 3 holds three half-waves**, and **Mode 5 holds five**. So the frequency multiplies by the number of bumps that fit, and the frequencies climb in equal steps.", "If the first mode is 100 hertz, the fifth mode is 500 hertz. If the lowest mode is 200 hertz, the fifth mode is five times 200.", "The numbers do not have to start at 100. A voice with a base frequency of **250 hertz** has its next harmonics at 500, 750 and 1000 hertz."], "end": "So **the fifth mode has the first mode fitted into it five times**, so it has five times the stiffness and the frequency.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture11/008.webp", "video": "assets/original/ac3e753564fa139e2612.mp4", "box": [0.0, 0.0, 100.0, 100.0], "poster": "assets/exam2/frames/ac3e753564fa139e2612.jpg", "caption": "Lecture 11 · p8 · with the video played on the slide"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 11 · p8 · Lecture 12 · p8 · Lecture 13 · p5", "main": "**A real string vibrates in all of its modes at the same time.** The separate motions add together into one complicated, irregular shape. That combined shape is what a plucked string actually does, and it looks a lot like the waveform of speech.", "sub": ["A single mode by itself makes a **pure tone**. A pure tone sounds so harsh that it is used for sirens.", "Normal sounds contain many modes at once."], "end": "", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture11/008.webp", "video": "assets/original/ac3e753564fa139e2612.mp4", "box": [0.0, 0.0, 100.0, 100.0], "poster": "assets/exam2/frames/ac3e753564fa139e2612.jpg", "caption": "Lecture 11 · p8 · with the video played on the slide"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 11 · p8 · Lecture 12 · p8 · Lecture 13 · p5", "main": "**A spectrum shows which frequencies are in a sound and how strong each one is.** **Frequency** runs along the horizontal axis. **Amplitude**, which is strength or how much vibration there is, runs up the vertical axis. Each spike is one harmonic.", "sub": ["For a string vibrating in all five modes, the spectrum has **five evenly spaced spikes** at 100, 200, 300, 400 and 500 hertz. The spikes have **equal height**, because nothing has filtered the sound.", "If the string vibrated **only in Mode 3**, the spectrum would have **one spike at the frequency of Mode 3** and nothing else. A list with one active slot and every other slot at zero is a **one-hot encoding**.", "The **vocal folds** are fixed on all sides, just behind the Adam's apple. So they behave as a fixed-fixed system and produce harmonics."], "end": "", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture11/008.webp", "video": "assets/original/ac3e753564fa139e2612.mp4", "box": [0.0, 0.0, 100.0, 100.0], "poster": "assets/exam2/frames/ac3e753564fa139e2612.jpg", "caption": "Lecture 11 · p8 · with the video played on the slide"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 12 · p10", "main": "**Making one spot on a string heavier lowers some modes and leaves others the same.** Suppose the very middle of a string fixed at both ends is made thicker, which adds mass there.", "sub": ["In **Mode 1**, the middle moves up and down a lot, so the added mass **lowers** the frequency. In **Mode 2**, the middle does not move, so the added mass changes **nothing**.", "In **Mode 3**, the middle moves again, so the frequency drops. In **Mode 4**, the middle is still again, so nothing changes."], "end": "So going up through the modes, the effect **alternates** between lowering the frequency and having no effect.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture12/010.webp", "video": "assets/original/ac3e753564fa139e2612.mp4", "box": [0.0, 0.0, 100.0, 100.0], "poster": "assets/exam2/frames/ac3e753564fa139e2612.jpg", "caption": "Lecture 12 · p10 · with the video played on the slide"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 12 · p10", "main": "**Along a vibrating string, movement and stretching take turns.** Where a piece of string moves the most, it is carried up and down and is hardly stretched. Where the string is stretched the most, such as right next to a fixed end, there is **no movement**. So **the most springiness is at the points with no movement**.", "sub": ["In **Mode 5** of a string fixed at both ends, the string does not move at the **two fixed ends**. It also does not move at the **four points where it crosses its middle line**.", "So those **six places** have the most springiness.", "The **most movement**, and the **least springiness**, is at the tops of the **five bumps**."], "end": "", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture12/010.webp", "video": "assets/original/ac3e753564fa139e2612.mp4", "box": [0.0, 0.0, 100.0, 100.0], "poster": "assets/exam2/frames/ac3e753564fa139e2612.jpg", "caption": "Lecture 12 · p10 · with the video played on the slide"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 11 · p11 · Lecture 13 · p7", "main": "**A system can be fixed at one end and free at the other end, and a building is an example.** A building is fixed to the ground at the bottom. It is free to sway at the top in strong wind or an earthquake. A string can be set up the same way, fixed at the bottom and free at the top.", "sub": ["The **fixed bottom** cannot move. A point with **no motion** is called a **node**.", "The **free top** moves the most. A point with the **most motion** is called an **antinode**.", "The first mode of this string is not a full half-wave. It goes from **no motion at the fixed end** to **the most motion at the free end**. That is only **one quarter of a wave**."], "end": "", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture11/011.webp", "video": "assets/original/50927605857bbe7450a9.mp4", "box": [0.0, 0.0, 100.0, 100.0], "poster": "assets/exam2/frames/50927605857bbe7450a9.jpg", "caption": "Lecture 11 · p11 · with the video played on the slide"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 11 · p11 · Lecture 12 · p14 · Lecture 13 · p8 · Lecture 14 · p4", "main": "**A fixed-free system has only the odd harmonics, because each mode is built from an odd number of quarter-wave pieces.** Each piece goes from no motion at a still point to the most motion at a moving point. **Mode 1 holds one piece, Mode 2 holds three, Mode 3 holds five, and Mode 4 holds seven.** So with a base of 100 hertz, the modes come at **100, 300, 500 and 700 hertz**.", "sub": ["Mode 1 goes from no motion at the bottom to the most motion at the top, as when a whole building sways.", "Two pieces stacked together give no motion, then the most motion, then no motion again. That puts a still point at both ends, which would make the system **fixed at both ends**.", "So two pieces can never fit. The next possible mode adds one more piece, for **three** in total, and that ends with motion at the free top again.", "When all four modes are equally strong, the spectrum measured at the free end has **four equal spikes**."], "end": "So the free end must always be moving, and only an odd number of quarter-waves ends on a moving point.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture11/011.webp", "video": "assets/original/50927605857bbe7450a9.mp4", "box": [0.0, 0.0, 100.0, 100.0], "poster": "assets/exam2/frames/50927605857bbe7450a9.jpg", "caption": "Lecture 11 · p11 · with the video played on the slide"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 11 · p11 · Lecture 12 · p14 · Lecture 13 · p7", "main": "**Engineers design buildings to sway only in Mode 1, with the whole building moving together.** In Mode 1 every floor moves together. On a high floor, Mode 1 feels unpleasant, but it is safe.", "sub": ["A building is not designed to be completely stiff because buildings that cannot bend or move slightly are more likely to be damaged during an earthquake.", "Engineers design buildings to never vibrate in a higher mode. In a higher mode, some floors would move one way and other floors the other way.", "Floors moving in opposite directions are dangerous, so the building could break apart."], "end": "", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture11/011.webp", "video": "assets/original/50927605857bbe7450a9.mp4", "box": [0.0, 0.0, 100.0, 100.0], "poster": "assets/exam2/frames/50927605857bbe7450a9.jpg", "caption": "Lecture 11 · p11 · with the video played on the slide"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 11 · p11", "main": "**The ends of a string decide which harmonics it has.** When **both ends are fixed**, the string has **every whole-number multiple** of the lowest frequency. When **one end is fixed and the other end is free**, the string has **only the odd multiples**.", "sub": ["Take a string with the **same length and the same wave speed**. Changing it from fixed at both ends to fixed at one end and free at the other **halves the lowest frequency**."], "end": "", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture11/011.webp", "video": "assets/original/50927605857bbe7450a9.mp4", "box": [0.0, 0.0, 100.0, 100.0], "poster": "assets/exam2/frames/50927605857bbe7450a9.jpg", "caption": "Lecture 11 · p11 · with the video played on the slide"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 13 · p8, 9 · Lecture 14 · p4", "main": "**The frequencies of a fixed-free system are called formants.** A **formant** is a frequency of vibration of a system that is fixed at one end and completely free at the other end. Formants are **odd multiples** of the lowest one, and they are written **F1**, **F2** and **F3**.", "sub": ["Counting quarter-wave pieces gives every formant from the first one. If **F1 is 500 hertz**, then **F2 is three times that, 1500 hertz**.", "**F3 is five times F1, 2500 hertz.**", "Each step between formants is twice F1, because each new mode adds two more quarter-wave pieces."], "end": "That is why the formants are odd multiples of the first formant.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture13/008.webp", "video": "assets/original/dbe01fd62c62e1105a6e.mp4", "box": [0.0, 0.0, 100.0, 100.0], "poster": "assets/exam2/frames/dbe01fd62c62e1105a6e.jpg", "caption": "Lecture 13 · p8 · with the video played on the slide"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 12 · p14 · Lecture 14 · p3", "main": "**The air inside the vocal tract is also fixed at one end and free at the other end.** At the bottom, the **vocal folds are closed** at the **glottis**, so the air there cannot move back and forth. So that end is **fixed**. At the lips, the air can move in and out easily, so that end is **free**.", "sub": ["A fixed end and a free end allow only the **odd harmonics**. So the frequencies of the air are one, three, five and seven times the lowest frequency.", "Unlike a building, the air in the vocal tract vibrates in **all of its modes**.", "Speaking changes the masses and springs of different parts of the air. That is how listeners tell vowels, consonants and words apart."], "end": "So speech uses two vibrating systems, the fixed-fixed vocal folds and the fixed-free air above them.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture12/014.webp", "video": "assets/original/50927605857bbe7450a9.mp4", "box": [0.0, 0.0, 100.0, 100.0], "poster": "assets/exam2/frames/50927605857bbe7450a9.jpg", "caption": "Lecture 12 · p14 · with the video played on the slide"}}], "questions": [{"source": "Review 7", "q": "A string is fixed on both sides. If Mode 1 has frequency 200 Hz, what frequency does Mode 5 have?", "a": "Mode 5 has a frequency of 1000 Hz. In a string fixed at both ends, each mode is a whole-number multiple of the first. Mode 5 contains five copies of Mode 1, so its frequency is five times 200 Hz."}, {"source": "Lab 9", "q": "A string fixed at both ends has Mode 1 at 150 Hz. What are Modes 2 and 3? If one end is freed and Mode 1 stays at 150 Hz, what are Modes 2 and 3 now?", "a": "Fixed at both ends, Modes 2 and 3 are 300 and 450 Hz, which are two and three times 150. Fixed at one end and free at the other, Modes 2 and 3 are 450 and 750 Hz, which are three and five times 150, because only the odd multiples occur. So the fixed-fixed modes are 150 Hz apart, and the fixed-free modes are 300 Hz apart."}, {"source": "Review 8", "q": "Why are the frequencies of fixed-fixed vibratory systems spaced the way they are?", "a": "The frequencies of a fixed-fixed system are evenly spaced whole-number multiples of the first, because every mode has to fit a whole number of half-waves between the two still ends. The first mode is half a wave, going from no motion at one end to the most motion in the middle and back to no motion. Mode 2 fits two half-waves into the same length, so it vibrates twice as fast. Mode 3 fits three, so it vibrates three times as fast, and the spacing stays even."}, {"source": "Review 16", "q": "Why are the formants of a fixed-free system 1, 3, 5, etc. multiples of the base frequency?", "a": "The formants of a fixed-free system are odd multiples because every mode must start still at the fixed end and end with the most motion at the free end. The basic piece is a quarter-wave packet, which runs from no motion at the fixed end to the most motion at the free end. One extra packet would leave a still point at the free end, which cannot happen, so packets are added two at a time. That gives one, three, five and seven packets, so the frequencies are odd multiples of the base frequency."}, {"source": "Predicted", "q": "If F1 is 500 Hz, what are F2 and F3?", "a": "F2 is 1500 Hz and F3 is 2500 Hz. The air in the vocal tract is a fixed-free system, so its formants are odd multiples of the first. So F2 and F3 are three times and five times 500 Hz, not two and three times."}, {"source": "Review 17", "q": "What aspect of speech has the properties in the fixed-free question?", "a": "The formants of the air in the vocal tract have these properties. The air is fixed at the closed vocal folds and free at the open lips. So its frequencies of vibration, the formants, are odd multiples of the first formant."}, {"source": "Predicted", "q": "What aspect of speech has frequencies at whole-number multiples, and why?", "a": "The harmonics of the vocal folds are at whole-number multiples. The vocal folds are a membrane held in place along their edges, so they behave like a system fixed at both ends. So each mode is a whole number of copies of the first."}, {"source": "Review 12 and 13", "q": "What in speech is a building analogous to, and why does the analogy hold?", "a": "A building is analogous to the air in the vocal tract. Both are fixed at one end and free at the other. The building is fixed to the ground and free at the top, and the air is fixed at the closed vocal folds and free at the open lips. Because the ends match, both have springiness at the fixed end, the most motion at the free end, and frequencies at odd multiples of the first."}, {"source": "Predicted", "q": "How is a building different from the air in the vocal tract?", "a": "A building is designed to sway only in Mode 1, but the air in the vocal tract vibrates in all of its modes at once. Engineers avoid the higher modes because they would move floors in opposite directions and could break the structure. In speech, speaking changes the mass and stiffness of the air at different locations, so the formants move."}, {"source": "Review 9", "q": "Draw the spectrum for a string fixed at both ends with Mode 1 at 200 Hz, up to Mode 6.", "a": "The spectrum has six evenly spaced spikes at 200, 400, 600, 800, 1000 and 1200 Hz, one for each mode. Frequency goes along the horizontal axis and amplitude goes up the vertical axis. The spikes are evenly spaced because a string fixed at both ends has its modes at whole-number multiples of Mode 1. If the modes are equally strong, the spikes can be drawn the same height.", "ansImg": "assets/exam2/gen-spectrum-fixed-fixed-200.svg", "alt": "Spectrum with spikes at 200, 400, 600, 800, 1000 and 1200 Hz"}, {"source": "Predicted", "q": "Draw the spectrum for a fixed-free system with Mode 1 at 200 Hz, up to Mode 4.", "a": "The spectrum has spikes only at the odd multiples, 200, 600, 1000 and 1400 Hz. A fixed-free system has no even multiples, because an even number of quarter-wave packets would leave a still point at the free end.", "ansImg": "assets/exam2/gen-spectrum-fixed-free-200.svg", "alt": "Spectrum with spikes at 200, 600, 1000 and 1400 Hz"}, {"source": "Review 10", "q": "If the vocal folds have their base frequency, F0, at 150 Hz, draw the spectrum for the first 5 harmonics.", "a": "The spectrum has spikes at 150, 300, 450, 600 and 750 Hz, with frequency along the bottom and amplitude going up. The vocal folds are a membrane fixed along its edges, so their harmonics are whole-number multiples of F0. So the spikes are evenly spaced, 150 Hz apart.", "ansImg": "assets/exam2/gen-spectrum-f0-150.svg", "alt": "Spectrum with spikes at 150, 300, 450, 600 and 750 Hz"}, {"source": "", "q": "How do two fixed ends decide which patterns a string can have?", "a": "**Only patterns that are still at both ends can fit the string.** Both ends must be nodes, because they cannot move. Whole numbers of half-waves start and end still, so those are the allowed modes."}, {"source": "", "q": "Why are the allowed frequencies whole-number multiples of the first one?", "a": "**More half-waves in the same length make shorter waves, and shorter waves have higher frequencies.** The wave speed stays fixed, so Mode N has N times the frequency of Mode 1. With Mode 1 at 200 Hz, Mode 5 is 1,000 Hz."}, {"source": "", "q": "Why do only odd numbers of quarter-waves fit a fixed-free system?", "a": "**Only an odd number of quarter-waves starts still at the fixed end and ends moving the most at the free end.** Each new mode needs two more quarter-waves to keep the free end moving. An even number would end at a still point, which is wrong for a free end."}, {"source": "", "q": "What do a neutral vocal tract and a building have in common?", "a": "**Both have one fixed end and one free end.** The air in the vocal tract is fixed at the closed glottis and free at the open lips. A building is fixed to the ground and free at the top. The materials and directions of motion differ, but the end conditions match."}]}, {"id": "local-change", "title": "Where the springiness is, and what a local change does", "lectures": "Lectures 12 and 13", "blocks": [{"ref": "Lecture 12 · p9 · Lecture 13 · p2", "main": "**Every vibrating system in speech follows the same short list of mass-spring principles.** The principles connect to each other, so they do not need to be memorized as separate facts. **Raising the mass lowers the frequency, and raising the stiffness raises the frequency.**", "sub": ["**A system with n masses has n modes of vibration**, and each higher mode has a higher frequency.", "Each higher mode is faster because more parts move against each other, so more springs stretch."], "end": "", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture12/009.webp", "caption": "Lecture 12 · p9"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 12 · p9 · Lecture 13 · p2", "main": "**A change in mass or stiffness matters in proportion to what that spot does.** **Adding mass lowers the frequency in proportion to how much that mass moves.** **Adding stiffness raises the frequency in proportion to how much that spring stretches and compresses.** Both effects depend on where the change is made.", "sub": ["A mass that does not move has no effect. A mass that moves a little has a small effect, and a mass that moves a lot has a large effect.", "**Where there is the most movement there is no stretching, and where there is the most stretching there is no movement.**"], "end": "So the location of a change decides which of the two effects counts.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture12/009.webp", "caption": "Lecture 12 · p9"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 12 · p9 · Lecture 13 · p2", "main": "**The way a system's ends are held decides how its frequencies are spaced.** In a **fixed-fixed system**, the frequencies are called **harmonics**. They are **whole-number multiples** of the base frequency, because the first mode fits a whole number of times into each mode.", "sub": ["In a **fixed-free system**, the frequencies are called **formants**. They are **odd multiples** of the base frequency, because the first mode fits an odd number of times into each mode."], "end": "", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture12/009.webp", "caption": "Lecture 12 · p9"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 13 · p2", "main": "**Raising mass and stiffness at the same time does not cancel out, except for a single mass on a single spring.** In a system with many masses and springs, a spot that moves a lot hardly stretches. A spot that stretches a lot hardly moves. So at any one spot, only one of the two changes matters.", "sub": ["At a spot on a string that never moves but is stretched hard, extra mass does nothing. Extra stiffness there raises the frequency, so **the frequency goes up**.", "At a spot on a string that moves up and down the most, extra stiffness does nothing. Extra mass there slows it down, so **the frequency goes down**."], "end": "", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture13/002.webp", "caption": "Lecture 13 · p2"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 12 · p11", "main": "**One small change to a string can make one mode lower and another mode higher.** Choose a spot where **Mode 4 moves the most** and where **Mode 5 crosses its middle line**. Mode 5 does not move at that spot. Then raise both the **mass** and the **stiffness** of that spot.", "sub": ["For Mode 4, the piece is moving, so the extra mass **lowers** the frequency. The piece is barely stretched, so the extra stiffness does nothing.", "For Mode 5, the piece does not move, so the extra mass does nothing. The piece is stretched the most, so the extra stiffness **raises** the frequency.", "So the same change **lowers Mode 4 and raises Mode 5**.", "Each mode is checked separately by asking whether the spot is moving or stretching in that mode. If it is moving, the mass matters. If it is stretching, the stiffness matters."], "end": "", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture12/011.webp", "caption": "Lecture 12 · p11"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 12 · p11", "main": "**Perturbation theory predicts how a small change in one location affects how something vibrates.** A drum is also made of countless masses and springs. In the late nineteenth century, researchers tested this by placing small amounts of **wax** on different parts of a drum. Cameras could not yet record fast motion.", "sub": ["When wax was placed where the drum vibrated a lot, the extra mass made the drum vibrate more slowly, causing its frequency (pitch) to **decrease**.", "When wax was placed where the drum did not vibrate, it had **no effect** on the drum's frequency or sound."], "end": "So the extra mass of the wax mattered only where the drum moved.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture12/011.webp", "caption": "Lecture 12 · p11"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 12 · p22", "main": "**The mass-spring principles explain every vowel, because each change only matters where it can act.** **Extra mass matters where the air moves.** **Extra stiffness matters where the air compresses and expands.**", "sub": ["The vocal tract is fixed at the glottis and free at the lips, so its formants are **odd multiples** of the lowest one."], "end": "", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture12/022.webp", "caption": "Lecture 12 · p22"}}], "questions": [{"source": "Review 6", "q": "This is Mode 5 of a string fixed at both ends. Circle the areas where there is the most springiness.", "a": "The most springiness is at the two fixed ends and at the four points where the string crosses its middle line, so six places should be circled. The string does not move at those points, but the pieces on either side move in opposite directions. So the string there is stretched and compressed the most. The tops of the five bumps have the most motion and the least springiness, so they are not circled.", "img": "assets/exam2/q06-string-mode5.png", "alt": "Mode 5 of a string fixed at both ends, with five bumps"}, {"source": "Predicted", "q": "This is Mode 3 of a string fixed at both ends. Where is the most springiness, and where is the most motion?", "a": "The most springiness is at the two fixed ends and at the two points where the string crosses its middle line, four places in all. The string does not move at those points, so it is stretched and compressed the most there. The most motion is at the tops of the three bumps, where the string swings the most and is hardly stretched.", "img": "assets/exam2/gen-string-mode3.svg", "alt": "Mode 3 of a string fixed at both ends, with three bumps"}, {"source": "Review 11", "q": "This picture shows Mode 4 of a building. Circle the areas where there is the most springiness.", "a": "The most springiness is at the fixed base and at the three points where the building crosses its resting position, so four places should be circled. Those points do not move, but the parts above and below them move in opposite directions. So the structure there stretches and compresses the most. The free top moves the most and is the least springy, so it is not circled.", "img": "assets/exam2/q11-building-mode4.png", "alt": "Mode 4 of a building with a fixed base and a free top"}, {"source": "Predicted", "q": "This diagram shows Mode 2 of a building. Where is the most springiness?", "a": "The most springiness is at the fixed base and at the one point above it where the building crosses its resting position. Those two points do not move, so the structure there is stretched and compressed the most. The free top moves the most, so it is the least springy.", "img": "assets/exam2/gen-building-mode2.svg", "alt": "Mode 2 of a building with a fixed base and a free top"}, {"source": "Review 14", "q": "If we increase the stiffness and mass of the string at the boxed location (where Mode 3 crosses its middle line and Mode 4 is at the top of a bump), what happens to Mode 3 and Mode 4's frequencies, and why?", "a": "Mode 3's frequency goes up and Mode 4's frequency goes down. In Mode 3 the boxed spot does not move but is stretched the most, so the added mass does nothing and the added stiffness raises the frequency. In Mode 4 the boxed spot moves the most and is not stretched, so the added mass lowers the frequency and the added stiffness does nothing.", "img": "assets/exam2/q14-string-modes-3-4.png", "alt": "Modes 3 and 4 of a string with a box where Mode 3 crosses its middle line"}, {"source": "Predicted", "q": "The box is moved to a spot where Mode 3 moves the most and Mode 4 crosses its middle line. Mass and stiffness are added there. What happens?", "a": "Now Mode 3 goes down and Mode 4 goes up. In Mode 3 the spot moves the most and is hardly stretched, so the added mass lowers the frequency. In Mode 4 the spot does not move but is stretched the most, so the added stiffness raises the frequency.", "img": "assets/exam2/gen-string-modes-3-4-box-middle.svg", "alt": "Modes 3 and 4 of a string with a box in the middle, where Mode 3 moves the most and Mode 4 crosses its middle line"}, {"source": "Review 15", "q": "If we increase the mass and stiffness of the building at its very top, what happens to the frequency of Mode 4, and why?", "a": "The frequency of Mode 4 goes down. The top of the building is the free end, so in every mode it moves the most and is hardly stretched. The added mass counts there and lowers the frequency. The added stiffness does nothing, because nothing is stretching at the top.", "img": "assets/exam2/q15-building-mode4.png", "alt": "Mode 4 of a building"}, {"source": "Predicted", "q": "If we increase the mass and stiffness of the building at its very base, what happens to the frequency of Mode 4, and why?", "a": "The frequency of Mode 4 goes up. The base is the fixed end, so it never moves but is always stretched and compressed. The added mass does nothing there because the base does not move, and the added stiffness raises the frequency.", "img": "assets/exam2/q15-building-mode4.png", "alt": "Mode 4 of a building"}, {"source": "Lab 9", "q": "In Mode 3 of a string fixed at both ends, point B does not move and point C is at the bottom of a bump. What happens to Mode 3 if you add a small weight at B? At C? If you stiffen the string at B?", "a": "A weight at B leaves Mode 3 the same, because B does not move in Mode 3. A weight at C lowers Mode 3's frequency, because C swings the most and the extra mass counts there. Stiffening the string at B raises Mode 3's frequency, because B is where the string stretches the most.", "img": "assets/exam2/gen-string-mode1-mode3-points.svg", "alt": "Modes 1 and 3 of a string, with point B a third of the way along and point C in the middle"}, {"source": "Lab 9", "q": "The same spot B is moving in Mode 1. Does a weight at B change Mode 1's frequency?", "a": "Yes, a weight at B lowers Mode 1's frequency. In Mode 1 the whole string is one bump, so the spot at B is moving. Extra mass at a moving spot slows the vibration, so the frequency goes down.", "img": "assets/exam2/gen-string-mode1-mode3-points.svg", "alt": "Modes 1 and 3 of a string, with point B a third of the way along and point C in the middle"}, {"source": "", "q": "Why does the effect of extra mass at one spot depend on the mode?", "a": "**Extra mass only matters where that mode is moving.** If the spot moves a lot in a mode, the added inertia slows that mode and lowers its frequency. If the spot is still in a mode, the extra mass has no effect on it."}, {"source": "", "q": "How can the same added mass and stiffness move two modes in opposite directions?", "a": "**The spot is mostly stretching in one mode and mostly moving in the other.** In the mode where it stretches, the extra stiffness wins and the frequency goes up. In the mode where it moves, the extra mass wins and the frequency goes down."}]}, {"id": "air", "title": "Air in the vocal tract: moving versus compressed", "lectures": "Lectures 12, 13 and 14", "blocks": [{"ref": "Lecture 13 · p10 · Lecture 14 · p5", "main": "**The air in the vocal tract is a fixed-free system that vibrates longitudinally, back and forth along the tube.** The tube runs from the vocal folds to the lips. The vocal folds are closed, so the air there cannot move, and that end is **fixed**. The lips are open, so the air there moves freely, and that end is **free**.", "sub": ["The air moves along the tube, not up and down inside the mouth.", "This air moves the same way a **building** sways, and the math for both is almost exactly the same.", "So the frequencies of the air are **formants**, which are odd multiples of the lowest one. **F1** is the lowest mode, and **F2** is the next mode.", "The modes look like a building's modes turned on their side. The fixed glottis end is on the left, and the free lip end is on the right."], "end": "That is why changing the mass and stiffness of the air moves the formants and makes different vowels and consonants.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture13/010.webp", "video": "assets/original/de37514880e11a8d6b49.mp4", "box": [0.0, 0.0, 100.0, 100.0], "poster": "assets/exam2/frames/de37514880e11a8d6b49.jpg", "caption": "Lecture 13 · p10 · with the video played on the slide"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 12 · p15", "main": "**The air in the vocal tract can be modeled as a chain of masses and springs.** The chain is fixed at the glottis and free at the lips. The masses stand for the air's **inertia**, which is its resistance to being moved. The springs stand for the air's **springiness**.", "sub": ["The lowest mode of the chain matches **F1**, and the next mode matches **F2**.", "The frequencies of a fixed-free system are called **formants**, and the frequencies of a fixed-fixed system are called **harmonics**.", "The second mode holds three quarter-wave pieces, so it vibrates **three times faster** than the first.", "The masses near the free end move very freely in both modes, because nothing holds them in place."], "end": "So the chain shows where the air acts as a mass and where it acts as a spring.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture12/015.webp", "video": "assets/original/de37514880e11a8d6b49.mp4", "box": [0.0, 0.0, 100.0, 100.0], "poster": "assets/exam2/frames/de37514880e11a8d6b49.jpg", "caption": "Lecture 12 · p15 · with the video played on the slide"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 12 · p23 · Lecture 13 · p11 · Lecture 14 · p6", "main": "**The air in the tube can also be modeled as lines of air particles, whose spacing shows what the air does.** Each particle stands for billions of real air molecules. The closed vocal folds sit at the fixed end, and the open lips sit at the free end.", "sub": ["Where the lines come very close together and then spread far apart, the air is **compressing and expanding**, so it acts like a **spring**.", "Where the lines move back and forth without changing their spacing, the air acts like a **mass**. This is easiest to see at the lips, where the air simply moves in and out.", "At the fixed end, the air cannot move, but it compresses and expands a lot."], "end": "So bunching and spreading means springiness, and sliding without bunching means mass.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture12/023.webp", "video": "assets/original/7bb6548efdc6fd9795ce.mp4", "box": [12.5, 0.0, 75.0, 100.0], "poster": "assets/exam2/frames/7bb6548efdc6fd9795ce.jpg", "caption": "Lecture 12 · p23 · with the video played on the slide"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 13 · p14 · Lecture 14 · p6", "main": "**In every mode, the air at the closed end is always springy, and the air at the open end is always moving.** Air can be compressed against a closed end. At an open end there is nothing to compress it against, so the air just moves back and forth.", "sub": ["In **Mode 1**, the air near the closed glottis end compresses and expands a lot. The air near the open lip end moves back and forth.", "In **Mode 2**, the air compresses a lot at the closed end and again about two-thirds of the way along. About one-third of the way along, the air mostly moves.", "In higher modes, springy places and moving places **take turns** more and more times along the tube."], "end": "So the first step for any vowel is knowing where the air moves and where it compresses in each mode.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture13/014.webp", "video": "assets/original/7bb6548efdc6fd9795ce.mp4", "box": [12.5, 0.0, 75.0, 100.0], "poster": "assets/exam2/frames/7bb6548efdc6fd9795ce.jpg", "caption": "Lecture 13 · p14 · with the video played on the slide"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 12 · p23 · Lecture 13 · p11", "main": "**The modes of the air match the modes of a building, and each higher mode adds one more point of no motion.** **F1** has one point of no motion, at the fixed end. **F2** has two, one at the fixed end and one inside the tube. **F3** has three.", "sub": ["Between the points of no motion, places of most motion and places of most compression take turns, just as on a vibrating string.", "A building in **Mode 1** matches the air, with lots of compression near the ground and lots of motion near the top.", "In **Mode 4** of a building, the springiest places are the four places that do not move. These are the fixed bottom and the three points where the building crosses its resting position.", "Raising stiffness where the building moves the most changes **nothing**, because it is not acting like a spring there. Raising stiffness where it does not move makes the frequency **go up a lot**."], "end": "So the points of no motion are the springiest places, because the air there compresses and expands the most.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture12/023.webp", "video": "assets/original/7bb6548efdc6fd9795ce.mp4", "box": [12.5, 0.0, 75.0, 100.0], "poster": "assets/exam2/frames/7bb6548efdc6fd9795ce.jpg", "caption": "Lecture 12 · p23 · with the video played on the slide"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 12 · p23 · Lecture 13 · p19", "main": "**The schwa, the central vowel with no constriction, matches the prediction for a uniform fixed-free tube.** A uniform tube predicts that F2 is three times F1 and F3 is five times F1. A measured schwa had formants near **500, 1500 and 2500 Hz**, which are those odd multiples.", "sub": ["Every other vowel moves the formants away from these schwa values, because it changes where the air moves and where it compresses."], "end": "So the schwa values are the starting point for describing every other vowel.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture12/023.webp", "video": "assets/original/7bb6548efdc6fd9795ce.mp4", "box": [12.5, 0.0, 75.0, 100.0], "poster": "assets/exam2/frames/7bb6548efdc6fd9795ce.jpg", "caption": "Lecture 12 · p23 · with the video played on the slide"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 12 · p18 · Lecture 13 · p15", "main": "**A constriction raises both the stiffness and the mass of the air, and the two effects do not cancel out.** Compressed air becomes **stiffer**, the same way pressing on a tire makes it stiffer. A constriction also packs the air into a smaller space, which raises its **density**, so the air is more massive and harder to move.", "sub": ["Canceling out only happens for a single mass on a single spring. In a system with many masses and springs, each spot in a mode is either mostly **moving** or mostly **stretching**.", "At a spot with no motion and lots of stretching, only the extra stiffness counts, so the frequency **goes up**. At a spot with lots of motion and no stretching, only the extra mass counts, so the frequency **goes down**.", "Speech proves the effects do not cancel, because constrictions in different places clearly change the frequencies of the voice.", "Adding mass and stiffness at the very top of a building **lowers every mode**, including Mode 4. The top is the free end, so in every mode it moves the most and is almost never stretched. So only the extra mass counts there."], "end": "That is why rounding the lips lowers every formant, because the lip end of the vocal tract matches the top of the building.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture12/018.webp", "video": "assets/original/de37514880e11a8d6b49.mp4", "box": [0.0, 0.0, 100.0, 100.0], "poster": "assets/exam2/frames/de37514880e11a8d6b49.jpg", "caption": "Lecture 12 · p18 · with the video played on the slide"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 12 · p15 · Lecture 13 · p15 · Lecture 14 · p7, 10, 14", "main": "**Any constriction question is answered by finding what the air at that spot does in each mode.** A constriction always adds both mass and stiffness. Where the air mostly moves, the extra mass counts and the formant goes down. Where the air mostly compresses, the extra stiffness counts and the formant goes up.", "sub": ["At a spot where the air in **Mode 3** bunches and spreads but hardly moves, the air acts like a **pure spring**. The extra mass does not matter there, so only the extra stiffness counts, and **F3 goes up**. The answer is built one step at a time, with each step resting on the one before.", "The same spot can be moving in Mode 1 and springy in Mode 2. Then one constriction lowers F1 and raises F2 at the same time, so each mode must be checked separately.", "Three spots along the tube matter for vowels. **/a/** is one-third of the way from the glottis, **/i/** is two-thirds of the way, and **/u/** is at the lips.", "At the lips, the air moves in both modes, so **/u/ lowers both formants**. Two-thirds along, the air moves in Mode 1 and is springy in Mode 2, so **/i/ lowers F1 and raises F2**. One-third along, the air is springy in Mode 1 and moving in Mode 2, so **/a/ raises F1 and lowers F2**."], "end": "So a vowel is identified by its pair of formants, not by one formant alone.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture12/015.webp", "video": "assets/original/de37514880e11a8d6b49.mp4", "box": [0.0, 0.0, 100.0, 100.0], "poster": "assets/exam2/frames/de37514880e11a8d6b49.jpg", "caption": "Lecture 12 · p15 · with the video played on the slide"}}], "questions": [{"source": "Lab 9", "q": "Why is the air in the vocal tract a fixed-free system? Say which end is which.", "a": "The glottis end is the fixed end, and the lip end is the free end. The glottis end is closed off by the vocal folds, so the air there cannot move and can only be compressed and expanded. The lip end is open, so the air there moves freely in and out without being compressed against anything."}, {"source": "", "q": "The curve in the vocal tract diagram goes up and down. Does the air move up and down?", "a": "**No, the air moves back and forth along the tube.** The height of the curve only shows how far the air moves at each spot. The curve is a graph, not the path of the air."}, {"source": "", "q": "Why can the air be strongly compressed at a spot where it hardly moves?", "a": "**Compression depends on how the neighboring air moves, not on how much the spot itself moves.** At a node, the air on both sides moves toward the spot and then away from it. So the air there is crowded and spread out the most, even though it barely moves."}]}, {"id": "vowels", "title": "Constrictions and the vowels /u/, /i/ and /a/", "lectures": "Lectures 12, 13 and 14", "blocks": [{"ref": "Lecture 12 · p16 · Lecture 13 · p12 · Lecture 14 · p8", "main": "**Vowels differ in the shape of the vocal tract, which depends on where the tongue or lips make a constriction.** A **constriction** is a narrowing of the tract. Images taken while people speak show where it occurs between the closed glottis and the open lips. Distances are measured from the **glottis**, the space between the vocal folds at the fixed end.", "sub": ["In **done**, there is no significant constriction, so the tract is fairly uniform along its whole length.", "In **dean**, the vowel is **high front**, because the constriction is under the roof of the mouth. That spot is about **two-thirds of the way from the glottis**.", "In **Don**, the vowel is **low back**, because the constriction is far back in the throat. That spot is about **one-third of the way from the glottis**.", "In **dune**, **lip rounding** makes the constriction **at the lips**, so the vowel is **high back**. In **Dan**, the tongue is low and toward the front, so the vowel is **low front**."], "end": "So the same voice from the vocal folds can become different vowels, because each constriction changes the formants.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture12/016.webp", "caption": "Lecture 12 · p16"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 13 · p13 · Lecture 14 · p9", "main": "**A constriction always raises both the inertia and the stiffness of the air at that spot.** **Inertia** is the air's resistance to being moved, which is its mass. **Stiffness** is how hard the air pushes back when it is compressed. In the mass-spring model, a constriction is drawn as **heavier masses and stiffer springs** at that spot.", "sub": ["Compressing air raises its stiffness, the same way compressing the air in a tire makes it stiffer. So a constriction where the air compresses a lot **raises the frequency**.", "A constriction packs the air into a smaller space, which raises its **density** and so its mass. So a constriction where the air moves a lot **lowers the frequency**.", "The two effects do not cancel, because moving places and compressing places take turns. At one spot in one mode, only the extra mass or only the extra stiffness counts."], "end": "So the effect of a constriction depends on what the air is doing at that spot in each mode.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture13/013.webp", "caption": "Lecture 13 · p13"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 12 · p21 · Lecture 13 · p13, 18", "main": "**Two questions, asked in order, answer any constriction question.** The first asks whether the air at the spot is mostly moving or mostly compressing in that mode. The second asks which change counts there, the extra mass or the extra stiffness.", "sub": ["On a string fixed at both ends, mass and stiffness are added at one small spot. At that spot, **Mode 3** crosses its middle line and **Mode 4** is at the top of a bump.", "In **Mode 3**, the string does not move there but is stretched the most. So only the extra stiffness counts, and **the frequency goes up**.", "In **Mode 4**, the string moves the most there and hardly stretches. So only the extra mass counts, and **the frequency goes down**.", "A formant change also tells what the air was doing. If a constriction raises a formant a lot, the air there must be **very springy and hardly moving** in that mode. If it lowers a formant a lot, the air there must be **moving a lot**."], "end": "", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture12/021.webp", "video": "assets/original/4362277e503aae5dbb25.mp4", "box": [0.0, 0.0, 100.0, 100.0], "poster": "assets/exam2/frames/4362277e503aae5dbb25.jpg", "caption": "Lecture 12 · p21 · with the video played on the slide"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 12 · p16, 19 · Lecture 13 · p12 · Lecture 14 · p8, 11", "main": "**In done, the vowel is schwa, and its formants are the starting point for every other vowel.** The tract has no significant constriction, so no extra mass or stiffness is added at any spot.", "sub": ["In **Mode 1** and **Mode 2**, nothing is added anywhere, so neither extra counts and **F1 and F2 stay at the uniform-tube values**.", "Schwa's formants are about **500, 1500 and 2500 Hz**, and the other vowels move the formants away from these values."], "end": "So each other vowel is described by how its constriction moves F1 and F2 away from schwa.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture12/016.webp", "caption": "Lecture 12 · p16"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 12 · p19 · Lecture 13 · p16 · Lecture 14 · p11", "main": "**For /u/, as in dune, F1 and F2 both go down.** **Lip rounding** makes the constriction at the lips, which is the **free end** of the tube. At the open end, the air moves back and forth with almost no compression in every mode, so it acts as a pure mass.", "sub": ["In **Mode 1**, the air at the lips is moving, so the extra mass counts and **F1 goes down**.", "In **Mode 2**, the air at the lips is also moving, so the extra mass counts and **F2 goes down**.", "The extra stiffness does not matter in either mode, because the air at the lips is moving, not stretching.", "A speaker went from the central vowel, near 500, 1500 and 2500 Hz, into /u/, and **both formants dropped**. A measured /u/ had an F1 in the low two hundreds, well below 500."], "end": "So at the lips only the extra mass matters, because the air there moves the most in every mode.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture12/019.webp", "video": "assets/original/ba7bea1a3c6655bda52d.mp4", "box": [0.0, 0.0, 100.0, 100.0], "poster": "assets/exam2/frames/ba7bea1a3c6655bda52d.jpg", "caption": "Lecture 12 · p19 · with the video played on the slide"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 12 · p20 · Lecture 13 · p17 · Lecture 14 · p12", "main": "**For /i/, as in dean, F1 goes down and F2 goes up.** The constriction is about **two-thirds of the way from the glottis**, near the front of the tract. That one spot acts differently in the two modes.", "sub": ["In **Mode 1**, the air at that spot moves back and forth with hardly any compression, so it acts like a mass. The extra mass counts, so **F1 goes down**.", "In **Mode 2**, the air at that spot compresses and expands a lot, so it acts like a spring. The extra stiffness counts, so **F2 goes up**.", "A speaker went from schwa into /i/, and the **first formant dropped a lot** while the **second formant went up**."], "end": "So the formants of /i/ **move apart**, because one constriction sits on a moving spot for F1 and a springy spot for F2.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture12/020.webp", "video": "assets/original/781434531c44b6e3d6b9.mp4", "box": [0.0, 0.0, 100.0, 100.0], "poster": "assets/exam2/frames/781434531c44b6e3d6b9.jpg", "caption": "Lecture 12 · p20 · with the video played on the slide"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 12 · p21 · Lecture 13 · p18 · Lecture 14 · p13", "main": "**For /a/, as in Don, F1 goes up and F2 goes down, which is the reverse of /i/.** The constriction is about **one-third of the way from the glottis**, toward the back of the throat.", "sub": ["In **Mode 1**, that spot is close enough to the closed end that the air is mostly compressed and springy. The extra stiffness counts, so **F1 goes up**.", "In **Mode 2**, that spot sits between two springy regions, so the air there mostly moves. The extra mass counts, so **F2 goes down**.", "A speaker producing /a/ showed **F1 going up and F2 going down**."], "end": "So the formants of /a/ **move toward each other**, and in a typical /a/ F1 and F2 are fairly close together.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture12/021.webp", "video": "assets/original/4362277e503aae5dbb25.mp4", "box": [0.0, 0.0, 100.0, 100.0], "poster": "assets/exam2/frames/4362277e503aae5dbb25.jpg", "caption": "Lecture 12 · p21 · with the video played on the slide"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 13 · p21, 22", "main": "**The spectra of /i/ and /a/ show the same harmonics shaped by two different filters.** The harmonics from the vocal folds are the same under both vowels. Moving the constriction moves the formants, so it changes which harmonics are loud.", "sub": ["In the /i/ spectrum, the tall harmonics are bunched at the very bottom. A long stretch of short harmonics follows, and the next tall group is much higher up. So **F1 is low and F2 is high**, and the two are far apart.", "In the /a/ spectrum, the first two groups of tall harmonics sit **close together** in the lower part. That is because F1 is raised and F2 is lowered."], "end": "So only the filter differs between /i/ and /a/, and the filter is set by where the constriction is.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture13/021.webp", "caption": "Lecture 13 · p21"}}], "questions": [{"source": "Review 19", "q": "If I constrict the vocal tract somewhere and I raise the formant a great deal, what must be true of air motion versus springiness at this location?", "a": "At that location the air must be very springy and hardly moving in that mode. A constriction adds both mass and stiffness, and the formant went up, so the added stiffness is what counted. Stiffness only counts where the air is compressed and expanded. The added mass did nothing, because the air there was not moving."}, {"source": "Predicted", "q": "If I constrict the vocal tract somewhere and I lower the formant a great deal, what must be true at this location?", "a": "At that location the air must be moving a lot and hardly compressed in that mode. The formant went down, so the added mass is what counted, and mass only counts where the air is moving. This is what happens at the lips for /u/."}, {"source": "Lab 9", "q": "A classmate says more mass lowers the frequency and more stiffness raises it, so a constriction always cancels out. What is wrong with this argument?", "a": "The argument is wrong because, in any one mode, only one of the two changes matters at most locations. If the air at the constriction is mostly moving, it is not being compressed, so only the extra mass counts and the formant goes down. If the air there is mostly compressed, it is not moving, so only the extra stiffness counts and the formant goes up. That is why the same constriction can push F1 and F2 in opposite directions."}, {"source": "Predicted", "q": "Compared with done, what happens to F1 and F2 in dune, and why?", "a": "Both F1 and F2 go down in dune. Lip rounding creates a constriction at the free end, where the air moves the most in every mode. So the added mass counts in both Mode 1 and Mode 2, and extra mass at a moving location lowers the frequency."}, {"source": "Predicted", "q": "Compared with done, what happens to F1 and F2 in dean, and why?", "a": "In dean, F1 goes down and F2 goes up. The constriction is about two-thirds of the way from the glottis. In Mode 1 the air there is moving, so the added mass lowers F1. In Mode 2 the air there is compressed, so the added stiffness raises F2."}, {"source": "Predicted", "q": "Compared with done, what happens to F1 and F2 in Don, and why?", "a": "In Don, F1 goes up and F2 goes down. The constriction is about one-third of the way from the glottis. In Mode 1 the air there is compressed, so the added stiffness raises F1. In Mode 2 the air there is moving, so the added mass lowers F2."}, {"source": "Lab 9", "q": "A new constriction is placed deep in the throat, one-fifth of the way from the glottis, where the air is compressed in both Mode 1 and Mode 2. What happens to F1 and F2?", "a": "Both F1 and F2 go up. The location is near the fixed end, where the air is compressed in both modes. So the added stiffness is what counts in both modes, and extra stiffness raises the frequency."}, {"source": "Predicted", "q": "Why does rounding the lips lower every formant?", "a": "Rounding the lips lowers every formant because the lips are the free end of the vocal tract. The free end is a location of maximum motion in every mode. So a constriction there adds mass where the air is moving, and that lowers every formant. Adding mass at the top of a building lowers every mode for the same reason."}, {"source": "", "q": "How do the tongue and lips change which vowel you hear?", "a": "**They change the location and shape of the air passage, which changes the resonances.** The tongue sets where the constriction is, and the lips can be rounded or not. Different resonances give different vowel qualities."}, {"source": "", "q": "Why can one voice source make several different vowels?", "a": "**The same source can pass through different vocal tract shapes.** The vocal folds are the source, and the vocal tract is the filter. Changing the filter changes the vowel, so no new source is needed for each vowel."}, {"source": "", "q": "Why does a constriction change more than one property of the air?", "a": "**A constriction changes both the inertia of the air and its stiffness.** Narrowing the passage changes how hard the air is to move and how it pushes back when compressed. So both effects have to be weighed within the pattern of each mode."}, {"source": "", "q": "A constriction makes a formant rise a lot. What does that tell you about the air there?", "a": "**The air there was mostly compressed and hardly moving in that mode.** A rise means the stiffness effect won. Stiffness wins where compression is strong and motion is small."}, {"source": "", "q": "Why does a constriction at the lips lower both F1 and F2 for /u/?", "a": "**The air at the lips moves a lot in both Mode 1 and Mode 2.** The constriction adds mass there, so it slows both modes. So both formants go down together."}, {"source": "", "q": "Why does the location of a constriction matter more than just calling it a constriction?", "a": "**The same constriction does different things in different places.** At another spot, the air may be moving in one mode and compressed in the other. So the word constriction alone does not tell you whether mass or stiffness wins."}, {"source": "", "q": "Why does the front constriction for /i/ lower F1 but raise F2?", "a": "**The constriction adds mass where Mode 1 moves and stiffness where Mode 2 compresses.** In Mode 1 the air there is moving, so the extra mass lowers F1. In Mode 2 the air there is compressed, so the extra stiffness raises F2."}, {"source": "", "q": "For /i/, what is the air at the constriction doing in Mode 1 and in Mode 2?", "a": "**In Mode 1 the air is mostly moving, and in Mode 2 it is mostly compressed.** So mass wins in Mode 1, and F1 goes down. Stiffness wins in Mode 2, and F2 goes up."}, {"source": "", "q": "Why does /a/ raise F1 and lower F2?", "a": "**At the /a/ constriction, the air is compressed in Mode 1 and moving in Mode 2.** The extra stiffness raises F1, because compression wins in Mode 1. The extra mass lowers F2, because motion wins in Mode 2."}, {"source": "", "q": "How does moving the constriction reverse the pattern of /i/?", "a": "**Moving the constriction changes what the air does there in both modes.** At two-thirds of the way, for /i/, the air moves in Mode 1 and is compressed in Mode 2. At one-third, for /a/, it is the other way around, so the formant changes flip."}]}, {"id": "source-filter", "title": "Source and filter, F0 and high voices", "lectures": "Lectures 13 and 14", "blocks": [{"ref": "Lecture 13 · p20", "main": "**Speech uses two vibrating systems at the same time, a source and a filter.** The **vocal folds** are a fixed-fixed system and act as the **source**, so they produce **harmonics**. The **air in the vocal tract** is a fixed-free system and acts as the **filter**, so its frequencies are the **formants**.", "sub": ["The lowest harmonic is the **base frequency**, or **F0**, and the other harmonics fall at whole-number multiples of it. In one example the harmonics are **100 Hz apart**.", "For the schwa, the formants are near **500, 1500, 2500 and 3500 Hz**.", "The vocal folds decide which frequencies are present, and the shape of the mouth decides which of those frequencies are loud."], "end": "", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture13/020.webp", "caption": "Lecture 13 · p20"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 14 · p15", "main": "**The mass and stiffness of the vocal folds set F0, and F0 sets the spacing of the harmonics.** More mass lowers F0, and more stiffness raises it. With an F0 of 150 Hz, the first five harmonics are 150, 300, 450, 600 and 750 Hz.", "sub": ["**Big, heavy, loose vocal folds** have a large mass and a small stiffness, so their **F0 is low**. It is around 100 Hz, with harmonics at 200, 300 and so on.", "**Tighter, lighter vocal folds** have a higher F0, such as 250 Hz, with harmonics at 500 and 750 Hz.", "**Very tight, light vocal folds** have an even higher F0, such as 350 Hz, with harmonics at 700 and 1050 Hz.", "F0 can be measured in **Praat** by viewing a spectral slice of a short piece of speech and picking the lowest harmonic. One measured voice had an F0 around 170 Hz, and the next harmonic was at about twice that value."], "end": "So a higher F0 spaces the harmonics farther apart.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture14/015.webp", "caption": "Lecture 14 · p15"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 14 · p15", "main": "**F0 keeps changing while people talk, and listeners use the changes to understand meaning.** Only a robot keeps F0 constant. Speakers change F0 by tightening and loosening the vocal folds with the muscles of the larynx.", "sub": ["When \"The dog slept here\" is a statement, F0 **falls** at the very end. When the same words are a question, F0 **rises** at the end.", "The way F0 moves also tells listeners whether a person is being sarcastic, pretending or even lying."], "end": "", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture14/015.webp", "caption": "Lecture 14 · p15"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 13 · p20 · Lecture 14 · p16", "main": "**The filter decides how loud each harmonic is, and a formant makes the harmonics nearest it loud.** All the harmonics are still there. **Harmonics near a formant are tall, and harmonics far from any formant are short.** The outline of the harmonic heights is the **envelope**, and it peaks at each formant.", "sub": ["During a schwa, harmonics near 500 Hz are loud, and the farther a harmonic is from 500, the weaker it is. Between two formants the harmonics get weaker, and near the next formant they get stronger again.", "The harmonics stay the same from vowel to vowel, and only their heights change. So the **envelope** is what makes each vowel sound different.", "Changing the vowel moves the formants while F0 stays the same. Changing F0 changes the spacing of the harmonics while the tract shape stays about the same."], "end": "So **pitch and vowel are separate**, because the source sets the harmonics and the filter sets the envelope.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture13/020.webp", "caption": "Lecture 13 · p20"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 14 · p16", "main": "**The formants of the filter depend on where the constriction is, and typical values show each vowel's pattern.** **Schwa** has F1 near 500 Hz and F2 near 1500 Hz.", "sub": ["**/u/** has F1 near 300, F2 near 1000 and F3 near 2000 Hz, so all of its formants are lower.", "**/i/** has F1 near 300 and F2 near 2200 Hz, so its first two formants are very far apart.", "**/a/** has F1 and F2 both near 800 to 900 Hz, so its first two formants are very close together."], "end": "So the vowel shape is the filter, and it chooses which of the harmonics from the vocal folds come through loudly.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture14/016.webp", "caption": "Lecture 14 · p16"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 13 · p25 · Lecture 14 · p17", "main": "**Formants can only be seen through the harmonics, so a high F0 makes the formants hard to see.** The formants form a smooth envelope, and the harmonics are samples taken from it. Suppose the harmonics are every **100 Hz** and the formants are at **300 and 2100 Hz**. Many harmonics then sit under each formant peak, so the formants are easy to see.", "sub": ["With harmonics every **250 Hz**, fewer harmonics land on each formant peak. A formant can fall **between two harmonics**, so it becomes very hard to tell the harmonics from the formants.", "With very light, tight vocal folds, a formant at 500 Hz may have **no harmonic near it** to make loud. So the formant can be missed or unclear.", "A higher F0 means fewer samples, so the spectrogram stops looking like clean formant bands.", "The formants are the same in every case, but a high voice gives the listener only a few harmonics to hear them through."], "end": "So the lower F0 is, the more harmonics the formants can shape, and the easier the spectrum is to read.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture13/025.webp", "caption": "Lecture 13 · p25"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 13 · p25 · Lecture 14 · p17", "main": "**Very high voices leave so few harmonics near the formants that their vowels are hard to identify.** The vocal tract still has its formants, but there are not enough harmonics near them to show the envelope.", "sub": ["A **toddler's** harmonics can be at 400, 800 and 1200 Hz. A **small child** of two or three has an F0 near 500 Hz. So only a caretaker who is used to the child understands the speech easily.", "A **soprano's** harmonics can start at 800 Hz and continue at 1600 and 2400 Hz. So the words of an opera singer are often very hard to follow.", "In opera singing the formants may not line up with any harmonic. Singers are even trained to shape the mouth so the formants line up with the harmonics."], "end": "That is why a toddler's or a soprano's vowels are hard to identify.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture13/025.webp", "caption": "Lecture 13 · p25"}}], "questions": [{"source": "Predicted", "q": "What does the source supply and what does the filter do in speech?", "a": "The vocal folds are the source, and they supply the harmonics, which are whole-number multiples of F0. The air in the vocal tract is the filter. Its formants make the harmonics near them louder and the ones far from them weaker. So the source decides which frequencies are present, and the filter decides which of them are loud."}, {"source": "Predicted", "q": "Two vowels are said at the same pitch. What is the same and what is different in their spectra?", "a": "The harmonics are the same, but the heights of the harmonics are different. The harmonics match because the vocal folds vibrate at the same F0 for both vowels. The heights differ because each vowel has its constriction in a different place, so the formants move and the envelope changes."}, {"source": "Predicted", "q": "Why are a toddler's vowels hard to identify?", "a": "A toddler's vowels are hard to identify because a toddler has a very high F0, so the harmonics are spaced far apart. Few harmonics fall near each formant, and a formant can fall between two harmonics, so the envelope is hard to see. The formants are still there, but there are too few harmonics to reveal them."}, {"source": "Predicted", "q": "If the vocal folds become tighter, what happens to F0 and to the spacing of the harmonics?", "a": "F0 goes up, and the harmonics are spaced farther apart. Tighter vocal folds are stiffer, and more stiffness raises the frequency. The harmonics are multiples of F0, so when F0 goes up they spread farther apart."}, {"source": "", "q": "What decides how far apart the harmonics are?", "a": "**F0 decides the spacing.** The harmonics fall at whole-number multiples of F0. So with an F0 of 150 Hz, the harmonics are 150 Hz apart."}, {"source": "", "q": "Why do the mass and stiffness of the vocal folds change F0?", "a": "**Stiffer folds repeat faster, and heavier folds repeat more slowly.** More stiffness gives a stronger pull back, so F0 rises. More vibrating mass has more inertia, so F0 falls."}, {"source": "", "q": "How do formants decide which harmonics are strong?", "a": "**The tract strengthens harmonics near its formants and weakens those farther away.** So the harmonics near a formant stand out as tall peaks. The envelope over the harmonic heights carries the formant pattern."}, {"source": "", "q": "Why are pitch and vowel quality two different things?", "a": "**Pitch comes from the source, and vowel quality comes from the filter.** Pitch depends on how fast the vocal folds repeat, which is F0. Vowel quality depends on the formants of the vocal tract, so each one can change without the other."}, {"source": "", "q": "Why can widely spaced harmonics hide a vowel's formants?", "a": "**Big gaps leave only a few harmonics near each formant.** The envelope is traced from the harmonic peaks. With so few peaks, the envelope and its formants are hard to see."}, {"source": "", "q": "If a formant is hard to see, does that mean it is gone?", "a": "**No, the vocal tract still has the formant.** It is hard to see because no harmonic falls close to its peak. Having too few harmonics near a formant is not the same as having no formant."}]}, {"id": "spectrogram", "title": "Spectrograms and speech over time", "lectures": "Lectures 13 and 14", "blocks": [{"ref": "Lecture 13 · p23 · Lecture 14 · p18", "main": "**A spectrum shows a sound at one instant, and a spectrogram lines up many spectra over time.** Speech is **time-varying**, because the tongue and lips keep moving and constrictions are made and released. Each moment of a spectrogram is one spectrum turned on its side.", "sub": ["**Time** runs along the horizontal axis.", "**Frequency** runs up the vertical axis, although it was on the horizontal axis of a single spectrum.", "**Amplitude**, which was the height of each spike, becomes **darkness**, so the louder a frequency is, the darker it looks."], "end": "So the dark bands are the formants, because the harmonics near each formant are the loudest.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture13/023.webp", "caption": "Lecture 13 · p23"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 13 · p23", "main": "**Formants appear as dark bands that move as the speaker moves.** In **/ia/**, the two lowest bands start **far apart** during /i/. As the speaker moves into /a/, **F1 rises and F2 falls**, so the bands **come close together**.", "sub": ["F1 is the lowest dark band, and F2 is the next one up."], "end": "So reading a spectrogram means following the dark bands as the formants change over time.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture13/023.webp", "caption": "Lecture 13 · p23"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 14 · p19", "main": "**In connected speech the formants never stay still, because every new sound moves the constriction.** In \"Where were you a year ago?\", the vocal folds supply harmonics the whole time. The tongue and lips keep changing where the constriction is, so the formants move from moment to moment.", "sub": ["Where the vowel sounds like **/i/**, **F2 rises high and F1 stays low**. That is because the constriction sits where Mode 1 is moving and Mode 2 is springy.", "During **you**, the formants **drop**, because rounding the lips adds mass right where the air moves the most.", "Through **a year ago**, F2 goes up and then down again as the tongue moves from one vowel to the next.", "Every rise and fall can be predicted with the same mass-spring reasoning. Find the constriction, then check whether the air there is moving or compressing in each mode."], "end": "So speech is not a string of steady vowels, and a spectrogram of a sentence is a moving record of the tongue and lips.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture14/019.webp", "caption": "Lecture 14 · p19"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 14 · p20", "main": "**Listeners identify a consonant partly from the vowel that comes after it.** A speaker said **ma** and **na**. The **m** part of ma was cut out and joined to the **a** part of na. The consonant came from ma, but **listeners clearly heard na**.", "sub": ["The vowel's formants differ depending on the consonant before it. After **m**, one formant of the vowel stays fairly **steady**. After **n**, that formant **drops sharply** at the start of the vowel.", "So the vowel carries the information about the consonant, and listeners decide which consonant they heard **during the vowel**, after the consonant is over.", "This was one of the first striking results in speech perception, published in the **1950s** by the linguist **André Malécot**."], "end": "So listeners take in whole stretches of speech and use later sounds to decide about earlier ones.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture14/020.webp", "caption": "Lecture 14 · p20"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 14 · p19, 20", "main": "**Speech recognition needs systems that take in whole stretches of sound over time.** The formants change constantly, so a network that sees two formants from one moment cannot understand real speech.", "sub": ["For about seventy years, speech recognition machines treated each slice of sound by itself, and they **failed** despite billions of dollars of effort.", "A machine that understands speech has to use the sound that follows to decide about the sound that came before. That is why modern speech recognition uses large language models built from many neural networks."], "end": "", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture14/019.webp", "caption": "Lecture 14 · p19"}}], "questions": [{"source": "Review 18", "q": "Describe a spectrogram.", "a": "A spectrogram lines up many spectra side by side over time. Time runs along the horizontal axis and frequency runs up the vertical axis. The darkness of each spot shows how much amplitude there is at that frequency and time. The formants appear as dark bands, so you can follow them as they rise and fall, for example F1 rising and F2 falling from /i/ to /a/."}, {"source": "Predicted", "q": "How is a spectrum different from a spectrogram?", "a": "A spectrum shows one instant, and a spectrogram shows how the spectrum changes over time. A spectrum has frequency on the horizontal axis and amplitude on the vertical axis. A spectrogram has time on the horizontal axis and frequency on the vertical axis, with amplitude shown as darkness. Real speech is always changing, so a spectrogram shows the formants moving."}, {"source": "Lab 9", "q": "A speaker starts on schwa and switches to another vowel. F1 goes down and F2 goes up. Which vowel? What if both go down?", "a": "When F1 goes down and F2 goes up, the vowel is /i/ as in dean, and the two bands move apart. When both F1 and F2 go down, the vowel is /u/ as in dune, because lip rounding lowers every formant."}, {"source": "Predicted", "q": "Describe the spectrogram of a speaker going from schwa to the vowel of Don.", "a": "F1 goes up and F2 goes down, so the two lowest dark bands move toward each other. The constriction for Don is one-third of the way from the glottis. The air there is compressed in Mode 1, so the added stiffness raises F1. The air there is moving in Mode 2, so the added mass lowers F2."}, {"source": "Predicted", "q": "Why do listeners hear na when the m of ma is joined to the a of na?", "a": "Listeners hear na because the information about the consonant is carried mostly by the vowel that follows it. After n, one formant of the vowel drops, and after m, that formant stays flat. So the a from na still signals n. Listeners decide which consonant they heard during the vowel, after the consonant is over."}, {"source": "", "q": "What can a spectrogram show that one spectrum cannot?", "a": "**A spectrogram shows how the frequencies change over time.** It lines up the spectra of one short stretch of time after another. A single spectrum covers only one moment, so it loses that change."}, {"source": "", "q": "What do the horizontal axis, the vertical axis and the darkness show in a spectrogram?", "a": "**Time is horizontal, frequency is vertical, and darkness is amplitude.** Each one has to be read separately. The darkest broad bands are the formants."}, {"source": "", "q": "Why do the formant bands keep moving in connected speech?", "a": "**The vocal tract keeps changing shape as speech goes on.** Each new shape has new resonances. So the formant bands rise and fall instead of staying flat."}, {"source": "", "q": "What do the formant transitions add beyond the middle of a vowel?", "a": "**The transitions carry information about how the mouth is moving and about the neighboring sounds.** A slice from the steady middle of a vowel leaves that information out."}, {"source": "", "q": "Why can the vowel from na change what you hear for the consonant from ma?", "a": "**The vowel from na still carries the transition from the /n/.** Listeners use that information from the vowel. When it conflicts with the consonant, the vowel can win, so listeners hear na."}, {"source": "", "q": "What does the splicing result mean for identifying speech one slice at a time?", "a": "**You cannot identify a sound from its own tiny slice alone.** What listeners hear depends on information that spreads across neighboring sounds. Classifying each slice on its own leaves that context out."}]}, {"id": "vowel-network", "title": "The vowel network: inputs, weights and decisions", "lectures": "Lectures 14, 15 and 16", "blocks": [{"ref": "Lecture 14 · p21 · Lecture 15 · p2 · Lecture 16 · p2", "main": "**A neural network can work as a simple speech recognizer that decides which vowel it heard.** It takes the **two formants**, **F1 and F2**, of a single vowel said on its own. The formants are measured from real recordings of five vowels. The network then decides which of **five words** it heard, **done, dune, dean, Don or Dan**.", "sub": ["This network copies a very simple piece of human perception, which is hearing one vowel and naming the word.", "Networks like this one were the kind used for speech recognition fifteen or twenty years ago.", "Modern **large language models** are built from many networks like this one, the way molecules are built from **atoms**."], "end": "So the whole job of the network is to turn two numbers into one choice among five words.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture14/021.webp", "caption": "Lecture 14 · p21"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 14 · p22 · Lecture 15 · p2 · Lecture 16 · p2", "main": "**The vowel network has an input layer, three hidden layers and an output layer.** There are **2 input neurons**, one for F1 and one for F2. The **three hidden layers** have **3, 2 and 4 neurons**, in that order. There are **5 output neurons**, one for each word.", "sub": ["The **input neurons** only take in numbers from the outside world. One speaker's vowel might have an F1 of 400 and an F2 of 1900.", "Information enters at the input, a little like sound reaching the ear. Each hidden layer turns the numbers it receives into new numbers, a little like the stages of processing in the brain.", "Every neuron connects to every neuron in the next layer, so the network is **fully connected**. The activations of the output layer become the network's decision.", "This first version is **purely linear**, which means it has **no activation functions**. Each neuron just passes on the result of its inner product."], "end": "So the numbers flow from 2 to 3 to 2 to 4 to 5, and the last 5 are the decision.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture14/022.webp", "caption": "Lecture 14 · p22"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 14 · p21, 23 · Lecture 15 · p2 · Lecture 16 · p2", "main": "**The network's knowledge is stored in its weights, and the weights are learned from the data.** Each connection has a **weight**, which is a positive or negative number. The whole network is a machine that processes numbers using those weights.", "sub": ["A **positive** weight makes its input push the next neuron up. A **negative** weight makes its input push the next neuron down.", "Before training, **every weight is a random number between minus one and one**.", "Because **the weights start out random**, the network knows nothing at first. Its output is usually very different from the right answer."], "end": "That is why the network has to learn before its decisions mean anything.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture14/021.webp", "caption": "Lecture 14 · p21"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 14 · p23, 24 · Lecture 15 · p3 · Lecture 16 · p3", "main": "**Forward propagation carries one input through the network, one layer at a time, until it reaches a decision.** The example input is **F1 of 320 hertz and F2 of 2000 hertz**. A very low F1 with a very high F2 is the pattern of **/i/**, so the correct answer is **dean**.", "sub": ["Each neuron computes an **inner product**, which means it multiplies each incoming number by its weight and adds the results.", "For the top neuron of the first hidden layer, the weight on F1 is multiplied by 320. The weight on F2 is multiplied by 2000, and the two results are added.", "The result of that inner product, with nothing else done to it, is the neuron's **linear activation**. Here it comes out slightly below zero.", "The first hidden layer produces three numbers. These go through the next weights to make two numbers, then four, and then five at the output."], "end": "So the calculations never need to be done by hand, because every step is just an inner product.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture14/023.webp", "caption": "Lecture 14 · p23"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 14 · p24 · Lecture 15 · p3 · Lecture 16 · p3", "main": "**At the end, the output numbers are turned into a probability distribution, which is the network's decision.** A **probability distribution** is a set of numbers between zero and one that **add up to one**. The step that makes this distribution is called **softmax**.", "sub": ["The only thing to know about softmax is that a bigger number gets a bigger probability.", "Probabilities and percentages are the same idea. A probability of 0.55 is a 55 percent chance.", "With random weights, the five outputs are about **.5, .1, .1, .1 and .2**, which add up to one. **Done gets about 56 percent**, which is more than half, and every other word gets much less.", "So the network decides it heard **done**, even though the input was dean. **With random weights, the network is wrong.**"], "end": "", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture14/024.webp", "caption": "Lecture 14 · p24"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 15 · p3", "main": "**All of the network's knowledge is positive and negative numbers, and all of its decision making is multiplying and adding.** Multiplying and adding are operations learned in second grade. **Inference** means working out a decision. The AI systems people chat with every day make their decisions using only these simple operations.", "sub": ["This is less surprising than it sounds, because brains are chemical machines.", "Inside neurons, the chemistry of ions such as sodium, potassium and calcium does something close to adding and multiplying."], "end": "So simple arithmetic, repeated across many weights, is enough to produce a decision.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture15/003.webp", "caption": "Lecture 15 · p3"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 14 · p24 · Lecture 15 · p6 · Lecture 16 · p2", "main": "**The network only improves when it is told the right answer, which is given as a desired output.** For a recording of dean, the **desired output** (the target) is **0, 0, 1, 0, 0**. That says there is a 100 percent chance the word was dean and zero chance for every other word.", "sub": ["A list with a single 1 and zeros everywhere else is called a **one-hot encoding**.", "The output the network actually produces is the **computed output**. The difference between the computed output and the desired output is the network's **error**, which is its **mistake**.", "The desired outputs come from real data, such as a table of each student's formants and the vowel that student actually said."], "end": "So the network learns by correcting its mistakes, and the error shows what each mistake was.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture14/024.webp", "caption": "Lecture 14 · p24"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 14 · p24 · Lecture 15 · p6 · Lecture 16 · p3, 7", "main": "**Backpropagation is the method that changes all the weights so the network makes better decisions.** To fix a mistake, **all the weights have to change**, both those near the output and those near the input. Backpropagation does this by combining **stochastic gradient descent** and **dynamic programming**.", "sub": ["**Stochastic gradient descent** is the same method used to improve an average from data, and here it improves every weight. It has the same **learning rate**, with the same tradeoff between learning fast and learning accurately.", "**Learning happens gradually, one small step at a time.** Each piece of data nudges every weight a little in whichever direction makes the error smaller, and the learning rate sets the size of each step.", "**Dynamic programming** decides the order, so the weights are updated **starting from the end**. First the weights into the output change, then those into the third hidden layer, then the second, then the first. The activations from the forward pass stay the same during this backward pass.", "The error says which way each output should move. Done, at about 56 percent, should go down, dean should go up, and the other words should go down. So after learning, giving 320 and 2000 again should make **done less likely and dean more likely**."], "end": "That is why AI needs huge **data centers**, because a modern chatbot can have **trillions of weights** to nudge over and over for months.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture14/024.webp", "caption": "Lecture 14 · p24"}}], "questions": [{"source": "Predicted", "q": "If an activation of 1.57 feeds the done output through a weight of positive 0.1, what happens to the probability of done if the weight goes up? If the activation goes up?", "a": "The probability of done goes up in both cases. The contribution to the done output is the activation times the weight, and both are positive. So raising either one makes the product larger and pushes done up. Only the signs matter, so no multiplication is needed."}, {"source": "Predicted", "q": "What happens to the probability of done if that same weight were negative and the activation went up?", "a": "The probability of done goes down. A positive activation times a negative weight is negative. So a larger activation makes the contribution more negative and pushes the done output down."}, {"source": "Predicted", "q": "Is 0.2, 0.3, 0.1, 0.2, 0.3 a valid probability distribution?", "a": "No, it is not a valid probability distribution. The numbers add up to 1.1, and a probability distribution must add up to exactly 1."}, {"source": "Based on HW5 5", "q": "The first of five categories is correct. Write the desired output.", "a": "The desired output is 1, 0, 0, 0, 0. It puts all of the probability on the correct category, so every other category gets zero."}, {"source": "Lab 9", "q": "The desired output over done, dune, dean, Don, Dan is 0, 0, 0, 1, 0, and the network outputs 0.10, 0.15, 0.60, 0.10, 0.05. Which vowel was said, which did the network guess, and what is the loss?", "a": "Don was said, and the network guessed dean, because dean has the largest probability, 0.60. The loss is a number that measures how far the computed output is from the desired output. For example, it measures how much probability the network put on dean where there should be a 0."}, {"source": "Based on HW5 3", "q": "What does Linear mean in the network code?", "a": "Linear means the layer computes inner products. Each neuron multiplies each incoming number by its weight and adds the results. A Linear layer does nothing else, so its outputs are just those sums."}, {"source": "", "q": "How do F1 and F2 work as inputs to the vowel network?", "a": "**F1 and F2 are the two measured numbers fed into the network.** The weights turn them into support for each of the five words. Each output position stands for one possible vowel."}, {"source": "", "q": "Why does a low F1 and a high F2 go with dean?", "a": "**A low F1 and a high F2 is the pattern for /i/, the vowel in dean.** So dean is the correct target for that input. That stays true even if an untrained network currently picks a different word."}, {"source": "", "q": "How many hidden layers does the vowel network have?", "a": "**It has three hidden layers, with 3, 2 and 4 neurons.** The layer of 5 neurons at the end is the output layer, not a hidden layer. The 2 inputs only supply F1 and F2."}, {"source": "", "q": "What decides how many weights go into one neuron?", "a": "**The size of the layer before it decides the number.** In a fully connected layer, each incoming value has its own weight. So a neuron after a layer of 4 neurons has 4 incoming weights."}, {"source": "", "q": "How does a neuron combine its weighted inputs?", "a": "**It multiplies each input by its weight and adds the results.** That sum is the weighted sum, also called the inner product. The result is one number."}, {"source": "", "q": "Why does the sign of a contribution depend on both the input and the weight?", "a": "**Both signs together decide the direction of the contribution.** A positive weight keeps the sign of the input, and a negative weight flips it. So a positive input with a negative weight pushes the neuron down."}, {"source": "", "q": "What happens during forward propagation?", "a": "**The input passes through one processing layer after another, using the current weights.** Each layer works on the results of the layer before it. The result at the end is the network's prediction."}, {"source": "", "q": "Why can a correct forward pass still give a wrong answer?", "a": "**The weights may be untrained or poorly learned.** The calculation can be done perfectly with bad weights. So a correct computation can still lead to a wrong decision."}, {"source": "", "q": "What makes a list of numbers a valid probability distribution?", "a": "**Every number must be zero or more, and all of them must add up to 1.** A list does not count just because a network produced it. It has to pass both checks across all the words."}, {"source": "", "q": "How is the correct target different from a prediction that is valid but wrong?", "a": "**The target marks the known word, but a valid prediction can favor a different word.** The target for dean has a 1 for dean and 0 for the rest. A wrong prediction can still add up to 1, so validity and accuracy are separate tests."}]}, {"id": "learning", "title": "Learning: blame, backpropagation and the training data", "lectures": "Lectures 15 and 16", "blocks": [{"ref": "Lecture 15 · p7 · Lecture 16 · p8", "main": "**For a last weight, one inner product connects the weight to the decision, so the direction of the change is easy to tell.** Take the last weight of 0.1, which connects the top neuron of the last hidden layer to **done**. That weight multiplies an activation of about 1.57, so raising the weight would raise the probability of done.", "sub": ["The desired probability of done is **zero**, so raising this weight would make the error **worse**.", "So this weight should be **lowered**. Lowering it shrinks its contribution and pulls the probability of done down toward zero.", "The same reasoning covers every last weight. The network said done when the answer was dean. So any last weight that pushes done up should be **lowered**, and any last weight that pushes dean up should be **raised**.", "A good answer says which way the weight should move and then explains why. It names the activation the weight multiplies and the output that the weight feeds into."], "end": "", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture15/007.webp", "caption": "Lecture 15 · p7"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 15 · p7, 8", "main": "**For a first weight, it is very hard to tell which way to change it, because its effect passes through every layer in between.** Take the first weight of 0.005 near the input. Changing it changes one activation in the first hidden layer.", "sub": ["That activation changes the second hidden layer, which changes the third, which finally changes the output probabilities.", "The effect of the first weight also **depends on all the later weights**, because the later weights come after it. So its effect on the error **includes the effect of the last weights**.", "So looking at the first weight alone cannot show whether it should go up or down. It is hard to tell how much an early weight is to blame for a mistake at the very end."], "end": "So backpropagation is needed to work out the right direction for the first weight.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture15/007.webp", "caption": "Lecture 15 · p7"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 15 · p8 · Lecture 16 · p9", "main": "**Backpropagation starts at the end because the last weights are the easiest problem.** The last weights decide the output probabilities directly. So the difference between the computed and desired output can be used right away to improve them. Improving the first weights is a much bigger problem that already contains the easy one.", "sub": ["**Dynamic programming** solves the small problems first and then reuses their answers for the bigger problems that depend on them. It was developed by **Bellman** and **Viterbi**.", "**Backpropagation is stochastic gradient descent plus dynamic programming.** Stochastic gradient descent changes the numbers gradually. Dynamic programming supplies the order, which is the **back** in backpropagation.", "So the last weights change first, then the weights before them, and so on back to the first weights. Each problem is easier because something was already learned from the problems solved before it.", "Blame for a mistake is easiest to assign to the weights closest to the mistake. That blame is then passed backward to the weights that came before them."], "end": "So the easy problem of the last weights is solved first, and its answer is reused for the harder problems of the earlier weights.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture15/008.webp", "caption": "Lecture 15 · p8"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 15 · p8 · Lecture 16 · p9", "main": "**Backpropagation is the method behind modern AI, and it was worked out in the mid-1980s.** **Geoffrey Hinton** won the **2024 Nobel Prize in Physics** for backpropagation, because there would be no AI without it. The idea is at the center of much of today's economy, and it has roots in physics through dynamical systems.", "sub": ["Modern systems use this same method on about **six trillion weights**, which is what the huge data centers are doing.", "Research from the last ten years suggests that single neurons in the brain also work forward and then backward, using their chemistry. So this forward then backward process may not be so different from what brains do."], "end": "", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture15/008.webp", "caption": "Lecture 15 · p8"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 15 · p9 · Lecture 16 · p10", "main": "**Training needs two tables of numbers, called matrices, and each row pairs one recording with the vowel it really was.** The **input matrix** has one row per recording and **two columns**, F1 and F2, because the network takes in two numbers. With about 110 recordings, the input matrix is **110 by 2**.", "sub": ["The **output matrix** has one row per recording and **five columns**, one for each word. A **1 marks the true vowel**, and the other columns hold 0, so the output matrix is **110 by 5**.", "Row by row, the network is told the right answer. Formants like 800 and 1700 go with one vowel, and formants like 700 and 1100 go with another.", "Each input row must stay paired with its own target row. If a row moved without its target, the network would learn the wrong pairing.", "During learning, the network picks a row, computes its decision and compares it with the true answer. If the decision is wrong, the weights change."], "end": "", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture15/009.webp", "caption": "Lecture 15 · p9"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 16 · p10", "main": "**If the network makes the right decision, there is no error, so it has nothing to learn.** This can be a real problem. A network can keep getting the right answer by **memorizing** its data without learning the real pattern underneath.", "sub": ["Stopping the learning at that point would be stopping too early, because memorized answers do not show that the pattern was learned."], "end": "So a right answer is not always proof that the network has learned.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture16/010.webp", "caption": "Lecture 16 · p10"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 16 · p10", "main": "**Children learning English show the same pattern, as Carol Chomsky observed in the early 1960s.** Their right answers come first from memory, and real learning shows up later as new mistakes.", "sub": ["At first, very young children say **go** and **went** correctly, because they are **memorizing** what they hear.", "Then they notice the real pattern, that most English verbs form the past tense by adding a sound at the end, as in **jump** and **jumped**. So they start saying **goed**.", "Later, they learn that some verbs are **exceptions**, so they get both the regular verbs and the exceptions right."], "end": "So a right answer early on can come from memory, and real learning can show up as new mistakes.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture16/010.webp", "caption": "Lecture 16 · p10"}}], "questions": [{"source": "Based on HW5 6", "q": "In what order are the weights updated?", "a": "The last set of weights is updated first, and the first set of weights is updated last. This order works because each layer's update reuses what was already worked out for the layers after it."}, {"source": "Based on HW5 7", "q": "Why does backpropagation start at the end?", "a": "Backpropagation starts at the end because the last weights are the simplest problem. The last weights affect the output probabilities, which are the decision, directly, so it is easy to tell whether raising or lowering each one makes the loss go up or down. A weight from the input to the first hidden layer affects the first, second and third hidden layers before the output, so changing it is a very complex problem that contains the problem of changing the last weights. Following dynamic programming, the simplest problem is solved first, and its answer makes each harder problem easier until the most complex one is solved."}, {"source": "Based on HW5 8", "q": "What two methods together train the network's weights?", "a": "The two methods are stochastic gradient descent and backpropagation. Backpropagation works backward from the error to find which way each weight should change. Stochastic gradient descent then moves each weight a small step in that direction."}, {"source": "Predicted", "q": "Backpropagation is made of what two parts?", "a": "Backpropagation is made of stochastic gradient descent and dynamic programming. Stochastic gradient descent nudges each weight a small step in the direction that lowers the error. Dynamic programming sets the order, from the last weights back to the first."}, {"source": "Lab 9", "q": "Which part of training is the SGD part, and which is the dynamic programming part? What are the small problem and the big problem?", "a": "The SGD part is the adjusting, where each weight takes a small step in the direction that lowers the error, and the learning rate sets the size of that step. The dynamic programming part is the order of assigning blame. The last layer goes first, and each layer's blame is reused for the layer before it. The small problem is the blame of the last layer's weights, and the big problem is the blame of the early weights."}, {"source": "Predicted", "q": "The network said done but the answer was dean. Should a last weight that pushes done up go up or down? Why?", "a": "That weight should go down. The probability of done is too high, because the desired output for done is zero. So lowering a weight that pushes done up shrinks its contribution and pulls the probability of done toward zero."}, {"source": "Predicted", "q": "If the network already makes the right decision on a recording, what does it learn from it?", "a": "It learns nothing from that recording, because there is no error to correct. This can be a problem, because a network can keep getting the right answers by memorizing its data without learning the pattern underneath."}, {"source": "", "q": "How do you tell whether raising an output weight helps lower the error?", "a": "**Raising it helps only if it moves the output the way the target needs.** Check the sign of the incoming activation to see which way the output score moves. Then check whether the target needs that output to go up or down."}, {"source": "", "q": "Why is the effect of an early hidden weight harder to trace?", "a": "**Its effect passes through every later activation and weight before it reaches the output.** So the final effect depends on all those later paths. Its own sign alone is not enough to tell."}, {"source": "", "q": "Why does backpropagation work out the later layers before the earlier ones?", "a": "**The effects of earlier weights depend on the later layers.** Once the gradients for the later layers are known, they can be reused for the earlier ones. That reuse is dynamic programming."}, {"source": "", "q": "What do dynamic programming and SGD each do in learning?", "a": "**Dynamic programming reuses calculations to find the gradients, and SGD uses the gradients to change the weights.** Working out the gradients is one step. Updating the weights is a separate step."}, {"source": "", "q": "Why must each row of formants stay paired with its vowel?", "a": "**The two rows describe the same recording, its measurements and its known vowel.** The network learns by comparing its guess with that vowel. So the rows must line up for the training to be correct."}, {"source": "", "q": "What would a mismatch between rows teach the network?", "a": "**It would teach the network the wrong link between formants and words.** One recording's formants would be paired with a different vowel. Each update would then push the weights toward that wrong pairing."}]}, {"id": "code", "title": "Reading the PyTorch code", "lectures": "Lectures 14, 15 and 16", "blocks": [{"ref": "Lecture 15 · p10 · Lecture 16 · p11", "main": "**A short Python program that uses the PyTorch library builds the vowel network and trains it.** The **import** lines bring in the **torch library**, which has ready-made programs for inner products, backpropagation and related work. So none of that has to be written by hand, and the network can be described in a few lines.", "sub": ["This works because of **libraries**. A library is a collection of programs written by one group that other people can use without knowing how they work inside.", "The idea of building software from parts like this goes back to **Margaret Hamilton's** software for **Apollo 11**.", "Around 2015, **Facebook** released **PyTorch** and **Google** released **TensorFlow**, so researchers no longer had to write their own backpropagation code. Most people now use PyTorch, which is often just called torch.", "The two **load** lines read the formant matrix and the matching vowel matrix from files. The formants become X, and the true vowels become Y."], "end": "So what matters is knowing what each line means, and writing the program from scratch is never required.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture15/010.webp", "caption": "Lecture 15 · p10"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 14 · p25 · Lecture 15 · p10 · Lecture 16 · p11", "main": "**PyTorch describes the network as a list of layers.** **Sequential** means the network is a **sequence of layers**, one after another, in order. **Linear** means the layer **does inner products** of its weights and the activations from the layer before, because a linear layer only multiplies and adds.", "sub": ["Each **Linear** line is one **processing layer**, which has weights and does inner products with the numbers coming in.", "nn.Linear(2, 3) means \"this layer receives information from 2 neurons, and it has 3 neurons.\" The next lines take 3 and give 2, take 2 and give 4, and take 4 and give 5.", "Setting the **bias** to False keeps every layer purely linear, which matches the diagram of the network. These lines only describe the network and do not train it.", "Every network covered so far is a sequence of layers. A large language model is an example of a network that is not just a sequence of layers."], "end": "", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture14/025.webp", "caption": "Lecture 14 · p25"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 14 · p25 · Lecture 15 · p10 · Lecture 16 · p11", "main": "**Only layers that do calculations get a line of code, so the input layer never gets its own line.** The **input layer** has no weights coming into it and does no inner products. It only receives numbers, so its only trace in the code is the 2 in the first line.", "sub": ["So the first line, nn.Linear(2, 3), describes the **first hidden layer**, and the **last line is always the output layer**.", "This network has **five layers in total** but only **four lines**, because the input layer does no processing.", "It has **three hidden layers**. \"How many layers\" and \"how many hidden layers\" are different questions, so each question needs a careful reading."], "end": "", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture14/025.webp", "caption": "Lecture 14 · p25"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 14 · p25", "main": "**To add a hidden layer of 3 neurons right before the output, only the end of the list changes.** The first three lines stay the same. The old last line, nn.Linear(4, 5), becomes nn.Linear(4, 3). A new last line, nn.Linear(3, 5), then takes 3 in and gives 5 out.", "sub": ["The first number on each line must match the second number on the line before it. That is because each layer takes its input from the layer before it."], "end": "So checking that the sizes match from line to line catches most mistakes.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture14/025.webp", "caption": "Lecture 14 · p25"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 14 · p25", "main": "**The same reading works for any network written this way.** Suppose the lines are Linear(4, 3), Linear(3, 8), Linear(8, 4), Linear(4, 4) and Linear(4, 5). That network takes **4 numbers as input**, has **four hidden layers** with 3, 8, 4 and 4 neurons, and chooses among **5 categories**.", "sub": ["Drawn as a network, it has **4 input neurons** in the first column. Then come hidden columns of **3, 8, 4 and 4 neurons**, and a column of **5 output neurons**.", "When the first category is correct, the target puts a **1 in the first slot and 0 in every other slot**."], "end": "", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture14/025.webp", "caption": "Lecture 14 · p25"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 15 · p10 · Lecture 16 · p11", "main": "**The last few lines are all PyTorch needs for learning, and they run one training step using all 110 rows at once.** **SGD** is stochastic gradient descent, and **lr** is the **learning rate**, set here to 0.1. The learning rate sets the size of each step.", "sub": ["The **loss** is the measure of the **mistake**. It compares the network's outputs with the true vowels, so the program can learn from its mistakes.", "**backward** runs **backpropagation** to work out how each weight should change, and **step** actually changes the weights.", "The old gradients are cleared before each step, because otherwise they would pile up from one step to the next."], "end": "So the program shows the network the data and measures how wrong it is. Then it works backward to find which weights caused the mistake and adjusts them a little.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture15/010.webp", "caption": "Lecture 15 · p10"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 16 · p11", "main": "**The learning rate trades speed against accuracy.** **A higher learning rate learns much faster but less accurately.** **A lower learning rate learns more accurately but much more slowly.**", "sub": ["So changing the learning rate from 0.1 to 0.3 makes the network **learn faster**, but its **accuracy goes down**."], "end": "", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture16/011.webp", "caption": "Lecture 16 · p11"}}], "questions": [{"source": "Review 20", "q": "How many hidden layers does this network have: Linear(2, 3), Linear(3, 2), Linear(2, 4), Linear(4, 5)?", "a": "The network has three hidden layers, with three, two and four neurons. It has five layers in all, the input layer, three hidden layers and the output layer, but only four Linear lines. That is because the input layer does no processing, so it gets no line, and the last line is the output layer.", "img": "assets/exam2/q20-sequential-code.png", "alt": "nn.Sequential with Linear(2, 3), Linear(3, 2), Linear(2, 4), Linear(4, 5)"}, {"source": "Review 21", "q": "Write the code for the network with an additional hidden layer of 3 neurons right before the output layer, and adjust the other layers.", "a": "Keep the first three lines, change the old last line from Linear(4, 5) to Linear(4, 3), and add a new last line, Linear(3, 5). The network becomes Linear(2, 3), Linear(3, 2), Linear(2, 4), Linear(4, 3), Linear(3, 5). This works because every layer still takes in exactly as many numbers as the layer before it produces.", "img": "assets/exam2/q20-sequential-code.png", "alt": "nn.Sequential with Linear(2, 3), Linear(3, 2), Linear(2, 4), Linear(4, 5)"}, {"source": "Predicted", "q": "Add a hidden layer of 6 neurons right after the input instead. What changes?", "a": "The first line becomes Linear(2, 6), and a new second line, Linear(6, 3), feeds the old first hidden layer. The rest stays the same, so the network is Linear(2, 6), Linear(6, 3), Linear(3, 2), Linear(2, 4), Linear(4, 5)."}, {"source": "Lab 9", "q": "A network is written Linear(4, 3), Linear(3, 5), Linear(5, 2), Linear(2, 4). How many hidden layers, inputs and outputs does it have?", "a": "It has three hidden layers, with 3, 5 and 2 neurons. It takes 4 numbers as input and has 4 output neurons, so it decides among 4 categories."}, {"source": "Predicted", "q": "How many hidden layers does a network have if its code is Linear(2, 3), Sigmoid, Linear(3, 2), Sigmoid, Linear(2, 4), Sigmoid, Linear(4, 5)?", "a": "It still has three hidden layers. Sigmoid lines are not layers, because they only apply the sigmoid to the layer before them. So only the four Linear lines count, and the last one is the output layer."}, {"source": "Predicted", "q": "What do the import lines do, and what does Sequential mean?", "a": "The import lines bring the torch library into Python, so the programs for inner products and backpropagation do not have to be written by hand. Sequential means the network is a sequence of layers, one after another. Each layer takes its input from the layer before it."}, {"source": "Based on HW5 9", "q": "What happens if the learning rate is raised from 0.1 to 0.3?", "a": "The network learns faster, but its accuracy goes down. A larger learning rate takes bigger steps, so the network approaches the answer quickly but keeps overshooting it."}, {"source": "Lab 9", "q": "Two learning curves differ only in learning rate. One rises fast and jumps around, and the other rises slowly and smoothly. Which used the larger learning rate?", "a": "The one that rises fast and jumps around used the larger learning rate. Bigger steps get near the answer quickly but overshoot, so that curve jumps around. Smaller steps are slower, so the other curve rises slowly but smoothly."}, {"source": "", "q": "What does nn.Sequential say about the path through a network?", "a": "**It says the layers run in order, each passing its output to the next.** Because of that order, neighboring layers must agree in size. The second number of one line must match the first number of the next."}, {"source": "", "q": "Why must the layer sizes match when you add a hidden layer?", "a": "**The new layer has to accept what the layer before gives and produce what the next layer expects.** If the sizes do not match, the values cannot pass through. Matching sizes keeps a valid path from input to output."}, {"source": "", "q": "What do the loss, backpropagation and the optimizer step each do?", "a": "**The loss measures the mistake, backpropagation computes the gradients, and the step changes the weights.** These are three separate stages of learning. Each stage uses the result of the one before it."}, {"source": "", "q": "Why are the gradients cleared before the next training step?", "a": "**Stored gradients would otherwise pile up across steps.** Clearing them lets each step compute fresh gradients for the current prediction. It does not reset the weights the network has already learned."}]}, {"id": "sigmoid", "title": "The sigmoid", "lectures": "Lectures 15 and 16", "blocks": [{"ref": "Lecture 15 · p11 · Lecture 16 · p12", "main": "**Artificial neural networks borrow their basic ideas from discoveries about real brains.** Real brains have **neurons**, and each neuron **gathers information from many other neurons**, which is what the inner product does. **Lord Edgar Adrian** discovered in the 1920s that **neurons are nearly digital**. He won the **Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine in 1932** for this discovery.", "sub": ["A real neuron is roughly **on or off**, either **firing** or **not firing**. It is not at some level in between like 2, 16 or 34.", "The neurons in the network so far are not like that, because their activations can be any number. Examples are slightly below zero, 6.64, 2.72 and 1.57."], "end": "That is why people added an activation function that makes artificial neurons nearly digital too.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture15/011.webp", "caption": "Lecture 15 · p11"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 15 · p11 · Lecture 16 · p12", "main": "**The sigmoid is an activation function that turns an inner product into a number between zero and one.** A **large negative** inner product gives about **0**, so the neuron is **off**. A **large positive** inner product gives about **1**, so the neuron is **on**.", "sub": ["An inner product of minus 4 gives almost exactly zero, and minus 6, minus 10, minus 100 and minus 1000 also give about zero.", "An inner product of **2** gives about 0.8 or 0.9. An inner product of 4 gives almost exactly one, and 6 or 100 also give about one.", "Only inner products **near zero** give values in between, and an inner product of exactly 0 gives 0.5. So the curve is steep in the middle and flat near both ends.", "The sigmoid makes the neuron **nearly digital**, like the chemistry that keeps real neurons from firing without limit. Without that limit, an activation of a thousand would be like lightning coming out of someone's head."], "end": "", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture15/011.webp", "caption": "Lecture 15 · p11"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 15 · p11 · Lecture 16 · p12", "main": "**The sigmoid also matches how perception works in animals and humans.** **Some things that are very different are perceived as the same, and some things that are very similar are perceived as different.**", "sub": ["A friend is recognized as the same person with a hat or without one, or in a different shirt on a different day. Perception ignores real differences, the same way the sigmoid gives minus 1000, minus 100 and minus 20 the same value.", "USC and UCLA are close to each other, both large, and alike in their labs and their football. Yet people perceive them as completely different, the same way two inputs just below and just above zero land on opposite sides of the sigmoid's jump."], "end": "So the flat parts of the curve treat very different inputs as the same, and the steep middle separates inputs that are almost the same.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture15/011.webp", "caption": "Lecture 15 · p11"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 15 · p12 · Lecture 16 · p13", "main": "**Adding the sigmoid to each hidden neuron changes every number that comes after it.** The inputs are still the formants. But each hidden neuron now passes on its sigmoid value instead of its raw inner product.", "sub": ["The first hidden neuron's inner product is slightly below zero, and the sigmoid turns it into about **0.4**.", "The second neuron's inner product is about 6.6, and the sigmoid turns it into about **1**, so that neuron is fully on.", "Each time a neuron changes, the activations in the later layers are recalculated, so the output probabilities shift.", "With the same random weights and the sigmoid on all nine hidden neurons, the choice moves from done to **Dan**. The network spreads its probability more evenly and gives Dan about one third, so it is **still wrong** about dean."], "end": "So the sigmoid changes how the network calculates, but only training with backpropagation can give it good weights.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture15/012.webp", "caption": "Lecture 15 · p12"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 15 · p13 · Lecture 16 · p14", "main": "**In the program, the only change is that nn.Sigmoid is added after each hidden linear layer.** nn.Sigmoid means \"apply the sigmoid,\" so it makes that layer nearly digital.", "sub": ["The **last layer has no sigmoid**, because its outputs go straight into the loss calculation. The loss calculation turns them into probabilities with softmax.", "The rest of the training program, with SGD and a learning rate of 0.1, stays the same."], "end": "", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture15/013.webp", "caption": "Lecture 15 · p13"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 15 · p13 · Lecture 16 · p14", "main": "**Training both versions and comparing them shows whether the sigmoid makes the vowel recognizer better or worse.** Other activation functions that look somewhat like the sigmoid, such as **ReLU**, can also be swapped in. Trying them out shows whether they make recognition better or worse.", "sub": ["**Many of the most basic ideas in AI come from how real brains work.** These include neurons, inner products that gather input from many neurons, and the nearly digital firing that the sigmoid copies.", "**AI copies abilities that animals have.** Its most basic ideas come from biologists' discoveries about brains, and computer scientists then add their own ideas on top."], "end": "", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture15/013.webp", "caption": "Lecture 15 · p13"}}], "questions": [{"source": "Predicted", "q": "Where does the idea of the sigmoid come from?", "a": "The idea of the sigmoid comes from biology. Lord Edgar Adrian discovered that real neurons are nearly digital, either firing or not firing, and he won the Nobel Prize in 1932. The sigmoid copies this by turning any inner product into a number between 0 and 1. So a neuron is nearly off or nearly on."}, {"source": "Lab 9", "q": "A hidden neuron's inner product is minus 2. Is its sigmoid activation closer to 0 or 1, and what does that mean?", "a": "Its sigmoid activation is closer to 0, because the inner product is negative. So the neuron is mostly off and fires only rarely."}, {"source": "Lab 9", "q": "If one input to that neuron goes up through a positive weight, what happens to its linear activation and its sigmoid activation?", "a": "Both the linear activation and the sigmoid activation go up. A larger input times a positive weight raises the inner product. The sigmoid always rises when its input rises, so the neuron fires more often."}, {"source": "Lab 9", "q": "What is the sigmoid activation if the linear activation is exactly 0?", "a": "The sigmoid activation is 0.5, which is halfway between off and on. So the neuron is half active."}, {"source": "Predicted", "q": "How does the sigmoid match human perception?", "a": "The sigmoid matches perception because some very different things are perceived as the same, and some very similar things are perceived as different. The flat parts of the sigmoid give the same output for very different inputs, such as minus 100 and minus 1000. The steep middle gives very different outputs for two nearly equal inputs on either side of zero."}, {"source": "", "q": "What does the sigmoid do to a negative, zero or positive sum?", "a": "**A large negative sum comes out near 0, a sum of 0 comes out as 0.5, and a large positive sum comes out near 1.** The output always stays between 0 and 1. The curve is S-shaped, so the response is nonlinear."}, {"source": "", "q": "Is a hidden neuron's sigmoid value a probability for a vowel?", "a": "**No, it is just a value between 0 and 1.** Each hidden value is limited on its own, but their total can be anything. A probability distribution must also add up to 1."}, {"source": "", "q": "Why do later values change when the sigmoid transforms a hidden activation?", "a": "**The later layers use the transformed value as their input.** If that input changes, their weighted sums change too. So their activations and the final decision must be recalculated."}, {"source": "", "q": "How does putting a sigmoid between layers change what the network does?", "a": "**It adds a nonlinear step between the weighted sums.** That changes the function the network computes. The change happens even before any weight is updated."}]}, {"id": "training-results", "title": "Training results: chance, overlap and repeated runs", "lectures": "Lecture 16", "blocks": [{"ref": "Lecture 16 · p5", "main": "**The real formant data shows a pattern, but it also shows a lot of overlap between the vowels.** Each recording is a dot, with **F1** on the horizontal axis and **F2** on the vertical axis. There are 525 dots, 105 for each vowel.", "sub": ["The **/i/ dots sit high**, with a **high F2 and a low F1**, as the theory predicts. So /i/ is clearly different from /u/.", "Many /æ/ dots sit among the /i/ dots, and many others overlap with schwa. The /u/, /a/ and schwa groups pile on top of each other.", "Some dots are probably measurement mistakes. Real data always has errors, the same way speech to a phone on a noisy train has errors, so these dots were left in."], "end": "So one pair of formants does not always point to just one vowel.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture16/005.webp", "caption": "Lecture 16 · p5"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 16 · p5", "main": "**If each vowel formed its own separate group, AI would be easy.** Because the groups overlap, a network has to see the data many times to learn where each vowel is most likely. Even then, many points are unclear.", "sub": ["If a new speaker's formants land in the middle of the overlap, the best answer is the vowel whose dots are most common there.", "Removing the differences between speakers is called **normalization**. People tried it for about seventy years without success.", "Hearing a lot of speech from the same speaker does help, because it gives the system more information about that person."], "end": "That is why the real formant data shows why AI is hard.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture16/005.webp", "caption": "Lecture 16 · p5"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 16 · p5", "main": "**This overlap between vowels was first discovered in the early 1950s by Peterson and Barney.** During the Second World War, spectrograms were kept secret by the military. The overlap was found once spectrograms became available.", "sub": ["Good speech recognition only arrived around 2011 or 2012, using neural networks trained by backpropagation.", "Surprisingly, speech that changes over time turned out to be easier for modern systems than this single-moment vowel problem. Large language models use information from before and after each moment."], "end": "", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture16/005.webp", "caption": "Lecture 16 · p5"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 16 · p15", "main": "**The full training program runs 2000 updates and records the accuracy after each one.** Each update uses all the recordings, with a learning rate of 0.1. The formants are first divided by a thousand, which turns hertz into kilohertz.", "sub": ["**Accuracy** is the percent of recordings the network classifies correctly.", "The word with the largest score counts as the network's answer."], "end": "", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture16/015.webp", "caption": "Lecture 16 · p15"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 16 · p15", "main": "**The accuracy starts at 20 percent, which is called chance.** With random weights, the network knows nothing. But with **five vowels**, even a random guess is right **one time out of five**, which is 20 percent.", "sub": ["With **ten categories**, chance would be **10 percent**, because a random guess would be right one time in ten."], "end": "", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture16/015.webp", "caption": "Lecture 16 · p15"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 16 · p15", "main": "**After a while, the accuracy jumps up to about 40 percent and then levels off near 45 percent.** The jump shows that the network is learning something. Each pass of learning is called an **epoch**, and a problem like this takes thousands of epochs.", "sub": ["This result is the accuracy on the training data, so it does not by itself show how well the network does on new speakers."], "end": "", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture16/015.webp", "caption": "Lecture 16 · p15"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 16 · p16", "main": "**Ten training runs from ten different sets of random starting weights all end at the same accuracy.** Each run used the same data. A starting set of random numbers is called a **seed**, though the word itself will not be asked.", "sub": ["The runs take **different paths**. One run stays at chance for about 500 epochs before jumping up, another jumps up very early, and another climbs more slowly.", "**All ten runs end at the same accuracy**, close to 45 percent. Stochastic gradient descent always involves chance along the way, but the end point is the same.", "Children learning language are similar. One child's home might talk mostly about sports, and another child's home might talk mostly about politics. By about age four, both children speak the same language, because the basic patterns under their different input are the same."], "end": "", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture16/016.webp", "caption": "Lecture 16 · p16"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 16 · p16", "main": "**The network levels off near 40 percent, not 100 percent, because the vowel data overlap so much.** For a point in the middle of the overlap, even a person looking carefully at the data would have to guess. AI is not magic, so a problem that is very hard for people is also hard for machines.", "sub": ["The next experiment is to add or remove layers, or to change the number of neurons in each layer. That shows whether the 40 percent gets better or worse."], "end": "", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture16/016.webp", "caption": "Lecture 16 · p16"}}], "questions": [{"source": "Based on HW5 10", "q": "Why does accuracy start at 20 percent?", "a": "Accuracy starts at 20 percent because the network starts with random weights and knows nothing. So its guesses are no better than chance. Guessing at random among five vowels is right one time in five, which is 20 percent."}, {"source": "Predicted", "q": "Why does accuracy start at 10 percent for a network with ten output categories?", "a": "Accuracy starts at 10 percent because a network with random weights guesses no better than chance. A random guess among ten categories is right one time in ten, which is 10 percent."}, {"source": "Predicted", "q": "Why does the vowel network level off near 40 to 45 percent instead of 100 percent?", "a": "The network levels off near 40 to 45 percent because the real formant data for the five vowels overlap a great deal. Many recordings with nearly the same F1 and F2 belong to different vowels. For a point in the middle of the overlap even a person would have to guess, so no network can classify those points reliably."}, {"source": "Predicted", "q": "Ten training runs start from different random weights. What happens?", "a": "All ten runs end at about the same accuracy, even though they take different paths. Some jump up early and some stay at chance longer, because stochastic gradient descent involves chance along the way. The end point is still the same."}, {"source": "", "q": "What does the overlap between vowels in the F1 and F2 plot mean for recognition?", "a": "**Different vowels can have nearly the same F1 and F2.** So those two measurements do not split the vowels into fully separate regions. For points in the overlap, some decisions will be wrong."}, {"source": "", "q": "Why does training accuracy only tell you about the training examples?", "a": "**Training accuracy counts correct decisions on those particular recordings.** It says nothing direct about recordings the network has never seen. So testing on new speakers needs a separate check."}, {"source": "", "q": "Why is one in five a useful starting point for five equally likely words?", "a": "**Random guessing among five equally likely words is right 20 percent of the time.** Only one of the five choices is correct. So 20 percent is the level a network must beat to show it has learned something."}, {"source": "", "q": "What does it mean when training accuracy levels off?", "a": "**The share of correct decisions has stopped rising in that run.** So more training is no longer helping. Leveling off below 100 percent means the network still makes mistakes."}, {"source": "", "q": "How can different starting weights give different learning paths?", "a": "**Different starting weights make different early predictions, so the corrections differ too.** Each correction depends on the error before it. So the path of learning changes, even on the same task."}, {"source": "", "q": "What does it mean that the runs end at the same accuracy?", "a": "**The end point is the same even though the paths differ.** The ten runs took different routes but reached the same training accuracy. Agreeing at the end is not the same as following the same path."}]}, {"id": "neurons-to-sequences", "title": "From one neuron to sequences: recurrent networks and LLMs", "lectures": "Lectures 17 and 18", "blocks": [{"ref": "Lecture 17 · p4 · Lecture 18 · p2", "main": "**All of modern AI is built from one simple idea, the artificial neuron.** A **neuron** takes in a vector of inputs from other neurons and gives back one number. It multiplies each input by its weight and adds the results, which is the **inner product**. The symbol **sigma** means add them up.", "sub": ["This multiplying and adding stands in for the chemical and electrical signals that real neurons send each other.", "A **neural network** repeats the inner product layer after layer, so it takes in one vector and gives back a whole vector of **decisions**.", "Each set of weights turns one vector into the next vector through many inner products.", "Even a decision as complex as a person's can be built from inner products and a function like the sigmoid, combined many times."], "end": "So people who move science forward often start from a very simple idea and repeat it many times.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture17/004.webp", "caption": "Lecture 17 · p4"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 17 · p2", "main": "**Artificial neural networks began in 1943 with a paper by McCulloch and Pitts, and that paper started from biology.** The paper proposed that simple math could model how the brain works. It started from the **all-or-none** behavior of real neurons, so a neuron is either firing or not firing.", "sub": ["All-or-none is the same idea as the **sigmoid**, so the very first neural network paper was built on how real neurons behave.", "Warren McCulloch was a **psychiatrist**, so the earliest work tried to model how the mind works, including mental illness.", "This early work was about understanding the mind and was not about making money."], "end": "", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture17/002.webp", "caption": "Lecture 17 · p2"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 17 · p2", "main": "**By 1986, these ideas had grown into the two Parallel Distributed Processing books, written by a group at UC San Diego.** **Parallel** means many neurons calculate their activations at the same time. **Distributed** means the information from the inputs is spread across all the neurons.", "sub": ["By 1986, forward propagation and backpropagation were well worked out.", "One contributor, **Geoffrey Hinton**, later won the **2024 Nobel Prize in Physics** for this work."], "end": "", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture17/002.webp", "caption": "Lecture 17 · p2"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 17 · p2 · Lecture 18 · p2", "main": "**A network like the vowel network takes one static vector and makes one decision, so it cannot model thinking that unfolds over time.** A **vector** is a list of numbers. A **static** vector is one fixed list from one moment, such as two formants that the network sorts into one of a few vowel categories.", "sub": ["**Speech changes all the time**, because every small moment has new formants.", "**Language also changes over time**, because a sentence is many words, one after another."], "end": "So one fixed list of numbers can never capture thinking that happens over time.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture17/002.webp", "caption": "Lecture 17 · p2"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 17 · p3", "main": "**Michael Jordan invented the recurrent neural network in 1985 to model how speech is produced over time.** **Speech production** is how the tongue, the velum and the lips move during speech. Features such as nasality, backness and lip rounding switch on and off at different moments across a sequence of sounds.", "sub": ["Jordan saw that a regular network could not handle something that complex, because it changes so much over time.", "The network is called **recurrent** because it **sends its own output back into part of its input**.", "So it produces something at one moment, uses that output to predict the next moment, and then the moment after that.", "The network is **not only sequential**, because it also has **two layers working side by side**."], "end": "", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture17/003.webp", "caption": "Lecture 17 · p3"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 17 · p3", "main": "**GPT works the same way as the recurrent network, one word at a time.** GPT takes the words of the prompt and predicts the next word, which becomes the first word of its answer. Then it feeds the prompt plus that new word back in and predicts the next word again.", "sub": ["GPT keeps repeating this, so the idea behind GPT began in 1985 with thinking about how speech unfolds over time.", "People kept improving the recurrent network from 1985 until **2017**, when the **large language model** was invented.", "Science and technology grow from other people's ideas over long periods. So the 1943 idea led to the 1986 networks, and Jordan's 1985 idea led to the 2017 LLMs.", "The people who came up with these ideas were mostly not the people who made a lot of money from them."], "end": "The years 1943, 1985 and 2017 give the history only.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture17/003.webp", "caption": "Lecture 17 · p3"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 17 · p4", "main": "**A large language model takes in many vectors, one for each moment in a sequence, and gives back one decision about what comes next.** Picture 15 vectors, each one standing for one word of a sentence. The job of the model is to **predict the sixteenth**.", "sub": ["Real systems take hundreds of thousands to a million input vectors, and this set is the **context window**.", "Each vector is also thousands of numbers long, so huge data centers are needed to run the calculations.", "The sixteenth vector is a **probability distribution over the next word**. English has about 30,000 words, so each entry gives the chance that one of them comes next.", "An LLM is made of neural networks, each set of weights is a **layer**, and real systems have about a hundred or more layers."], "end": "So an LLM's knowledge, such as how to write an essay, is still stored in its **weights**.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture17/004.webp", "caption": "Lecture 17 · p4"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 17 · p4 · Lecture 18 · p2", "main": "**An LLM learns by predicting the next word and correcting its mistakes.** In \"the cat sat on the,\" the real next word is **mat**. A new LLM with random weights might predict an impossible word like \"in.\"", "sub": ["**Backpropagation** then adjusts the trillions of weights, so the prediction moves toward mat.", "After this is repeated with about a trillion sentences, the system can speak English.", "No philosopher or cognitive scientist expected that human language could be copied with only inner products and functions like the sigmoid.", "The first problem this design solved, in 2017, was **machine translation**, and the same design then worked for speech and many other problems."], "end": "", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture17/004.webp", "caption": "Lecture 17 · p4"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 18 · p2", "main": "**Language depends on connections between words that are far apart, and an LLM learns those connections from data.** In \"the dog that my parents, who I love very much, who live in a state far, far away,\" the next word is **barks**. It is not bark. **Barks** goes with the singular word **dog**, no matter how many words come in between.", "sub": ["The plural word parents sits in between, but the verb still has to match dog.", "Nobody teaches this rule directly, because speakers pick it up from the language around them.", "An LLM learns it the same way, by doing inner products layer after layer until it predicts barks.", "English sentence structure, called **syntax**, is so complicated that many linguists study it for whole careers, so an LLM needs many layers."], "end": "", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture18/002.webp", "caption": "Lecture 18 · p2"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 18 · p2", "main": "**LLMs can work across many languages and many kinds of input.** **Translation** between languages was the first use of LLMs, and it is now nearly solved for hundreds of languages. A system trained on several languages, such as English, Dutch and Japanese, learns how words go together in general.", "sub": ["So that system also gets good at very different languages, such as Navajo.", "A **multimodal** system connects different kinds of input, such as text, speech and images.", "So a multimodal system can write a caption for a photo or create an image from a spoken description."], "end": "", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture18/002.webp", "caption": "Lecture 18 · p2"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 17 · p5 · Lecture 18 · p5", "main": "**An LLM is given 15 vectors and asked to predict the 16th, and the vectors can stand for almost anything.** A vector is just a list of numbers, so the machine plays the same prediction game whatever the numbers mean. The vectors can be **15 words** of a sentence, with the next word missing.", "sub": ["One example sentence is \"The cat that the dog chased ran up the old tree and sat on the.\" A speaker of English knows a likely next word, such as branch or mat.", "Getting it right means using the whole sentence, because the cat sat, not the dog.", "The vectors can be **15 frames of a spectrogram**, where each small frame is one vector of frequencies and amplitudes. The system then predicts the next slice of speech.", "In 2019 this method gave the **best speech recognition in 75 years**. It is also the basis for computer voices that are very hard to tell apart from humans."], "end": "The newest systems can hold a spoken conversation, and the only clue left is a short delay before each response. As that delay shrinks, phone scams become a real danger.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture17/005.webp", "caption": "Lecture 17 · p5"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 17 · p5 · Lecture 18 · p5", "main": "**The same prediction game works for images and for proteins.** An image can be cut into **16 patches** and read row by row. The system is given 15 patches and predicts the missing bottom-right patch.", "sub": ["After training on huge numbers of images, the system learns what a dog, a dolphin or a boat is. This became the basis of modern **computer vision**.", "A protein is a chain of amino acids, which come in **20 kinds**. The order of the chain decides how the protein folds into its shape.", "In the B chain of insulin, the amino acid after the first fifteen is the one written Y.", "Treating a protein as a sequence moved the **protein folding problem** many years ahead, which helps with designing drugs and vaccines. That work won the **2024 Nobel Prize in Chemistry**."], "end": "", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture17/005.webp", "caption": "Lecture 17 · p5"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 17 · p5 · Lecture 18 · p5", "main": "**The LLM only ever sees vectors, so one design works for language, speech, images and proteins.** Words, sounds, images and molecules all reach the LLM as lists of numbers. So the same design asks the same question every time, which is what comes sixteenth.", "sub": ["These systems predict from past data, so something new that the past data does not cover can make them **hallucinate**.", "A prediction far into an unfamiliar future can end up as a guess, or as an honest \"I don't know.\""], "end": "", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture17/005.webp", "caption": "Lecture 17 · p5"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 18 · p2", "main": "**Nobody fully understands what all the weights in an LLM are doing.** The designs were not planned from the start. Over about 75 years, people changed something that worked a little so that it worked a little better, again and again.", "sub": ["Dogs were bred into today's breeds in a similar way over a few hundred years.", "Researchers are now trying to figure out what the weights do, partly so that smaller and less wasteful systems can be built."], "end": "", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture18/002.webp", "caption": "Lecture 18 · p2"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 17 · p4", "main": "**The main idea behind all of this is Gestalt completion.** People see a triangle in the Gestalt figure even though no triangle is drawn. They see it because much of human knowledge is **completing** what is missing. So training a system only to **predict the missing piece** turns out to copy human thinking.", "sub": ["The **cloze procedure** is a test introduced by **Wilson Taylor** in **1953**, where words are removed from a text and people fill them in.", "The name comes from **closure**, the Gestalt word for completing a familiar pattern that is not quite finished. Seeing a broken circle as whole is closure.", "Knowledge can be tested by asking someone to complete a sequence, and for AI, **completion is all that knowledge is**.", "Underneath everything, **inference is inner products**, and **learning is stochastic gradient descent plus dynamic programming**."], "end": "So an LLM predicting the next word is playing a cloze game again and again.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture17/004.webp", "caption": "Lecture 17 · p4"}}], "questions": [{"source": "Predicted", "q": "How does the all-or-none behavior of neurons connect to the sigmoid?", "a": "The all-or-none behavior of neurons and the sigmoid are the same idea. A real neuron is either firing or not firing. The sigmoid turns an inner product into a number near 0 (off) or near 1 (on), with a steep change in between, so an artificial neuron behaves the same way."}, {"source": "Predicted", "q": "What do parallel and distributed mean in Parallel Distributed Processing?", "a": "Parallel means many neurons calculate their activations at the same time. Distributed means the information from the inputs is spread across all the neurons, so it is not stored in one place."}, {"source": "Predicted", "q": "What does a single neuron take in and give back? What about a neural network?", "a": "A single neuron takes in a vector and gives back one number, the inner product of the inputs and their weights. A neural network repeats the inner product layer after layer. So a network takes in one vector and gives back a vector of decisions."}, {"source": "Predicted", "q": "Why can't the vowel network model a sentence or speech over time?", "a": "The vowel network cannot model speech over time because it takes one static vector and makes one decision. That vector is F1 and F2 from one moment. Speech changes all the time, and a sentence is many words one after another, so it needs a network that works through a sequence."}, {"source": "Predicted", "q": "What makes Jordan's network recurrent, and why did he build it?", "a": "Jordan's network is recurrent because it sends its own output back into part of its input. It copies the output into the state units, so what it produced at one moment helps predict the next. Jordan built it because speech production changes over time, as features such as nasality and lip rounding switch on and off."}, {"source": "Predicted", "q": "How is a recurrent network different from an nn.Sequential network?", "a": "A Sequential network passes the input through the layers once, from the input to the output, and makes one decision. A recurrent network loops its output back into its input, so it works through a sequence step by step."}, {"source": "Predicted", "q": "How does GPT use the same idea as Jordan's network?", "a": "GPT also feeds its own output back into its input. It predicts the next word, adds that word to the prompt, and predicts again, so it produces text one word at a time."}, {"source": "Lab 9", "q": "What is the difference between the input and output of the vowel network and those of an LLM?", "a": "The vowel network takes one vector, F1 and F2, and decides among five categories. An LLM takes many vectors in sequence, such as the words so far, and decides among a whole vocabulary for the next item."}, {"source": "Predicted", "q": "An LLM is given 15 vectors and predicts the sixteenth. Name three things the vectors can stand for.", "a": "The vectors can stand for the words of a sentence, the frames of a spectrogram or the patches of an image. They can also stand for the amino acids of a protein. The same kind of prediction works for all of them, because the LLM only sees lists of numbers."}, {"source": "Predicted", "q": "What does the sixteenth vector look like, and does it add up to anything?", "a": "The sixteenth vector is a probability distribution over what could come next. It has one number for each possible next item, such as each English word. Because it is a probability distribution, its numbers add up to one."}, {"source": "Predicted", "q": "Why is the next word barks and not bark in \"the dog that my parents, who I love very much, ... \"?", "a": "The next word is barks because it goes with the singular word dog, no matter how many words come in between. This holds even when the plural word parents comes in between. To predict barks, an LLM has to use a word far back in the sequence, and it learns to do this from data by computing inner products layer after layer."}, {"source": "Predicted", "q": "Why can an LLM hallucinate?", "a": "An LLM can hallucinate because it predicts from past data. When it meets something its data does not cover, its prediction can turn into a guess, so it can make things up."}, {"source": "Predicted", "q": "Describe the Gestalt principle of closure, and how an LLM uses it to learn.", "a": "Closure is completing a familiar pattern that is not quite finished, such as seeing a triangle where none is drawn or seeing a broken circle as whole. An LLM learns by doing the same kind of completion. It predicts the missing next piece of a sequence again and again, and it corrects its weights when it is wrong."}, {"source": "Predicted", "q": "What is a cloze test, and why is an LLM's training like one?", "a": "In a cloze test, words are removed from a text and people fill them in, so knowledge is tested by completing a sequence. An LLM is trained by filling in the next word over and over. So for an LLM, completion is what knowledge is."}, {"source": "Predicted", "q": "Why do people see a triangle in the Gestalt figure?", "a": "People see a triangle because they complete the missing parts of a familiar pattern, even though no triangle is drawn. This completion is the Gestalt principle of closure.", "img": "assets/exam2/gen-kanizsa-triangle.svg", "alt": "Three black circles with wedges cut out, facing each other"}, {"source": "", "q": "What does a model neuron simplify about a real neuron?", "a": "**It keeps the links between incoming activity, connection strengths and the response, and drops the biological details.** The weighted sum stands for how the neuron gathers its inputs. The chemistry and electricity of a real neuron are left out."}, {"source": "", "q": "Why should a model inspired by the brain not be treated as a copy of the brain?", "a": "**A model can be useful without copying every physical detail.** It only keeps one part of what a real neuron does. So the model needs its own evidence that it works."}, {"source": "", "q": "How does a recurrent network let earlier items matter?", "a": "**It carries its state from one step to the next by feeding its output back in.** What it produced at one moment becomes part of its input at the next. So its current activity depends on earlier context."}, {"source": "", "q": "Why does a sequence need context and not just one static input?", "a": "**What an item is, or what comes after it, can depend on the items before it.** Processing each item alone throws away those relationships. So a network for sequences has to carry context forward."}, {"source": "", "q": "How are a neuron, a network and an agentic system different levels?", "a": "**Each level is built from the one below it.** A neuron combines inputs, and a network combines processing layers. An agentic system connects whole LLMs that each have a task."}, {"source": "", "q": "Why do the lower-level computations still matter in a large agentic system?", "a": "**The higher levels are built out of the lower computations.** An agentic system is made of LLMs. Each LLM still runs on the weighted sums inside its layers."}, {"source": "", "q": "What stays the same when an LLM predicts words, sounds or images?", "a": "**The task stays the same, which is predicting the next vector from the ones before.** Every kind of item is turned into an ordered list of vectors. The material can differ without changing the task."}, {"source": "", "q": "How do the items so far shape the probabilities for the next one?", "a": "**The context changes which next items fit and how likely each one is.** When many items can follow, the probability is spread out. When few can follow, it piles onto those few."}]}, {"id": "heads-depth", "title": "Heads, layers and the LLM as a dynamical system", "lectures": "Lectures 17 and 18", "blocks": [{"ref": "Lecture 18 · p3", "main": "**An LLM is built from layers, and each layer contains many heads working side by side.** Predicting the next word is so hard that the model needs both. A **head** is one expert, and the knowledge from all the heads in a layer is combined to make **improved vectors**.", "sub": ["**Layers go one after another from the input to the output.**", "**Heads sit side by side inside each layer.**", "The next layer then takes those improved vectors and does the same thing again."], "end": "", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture18/003.webp", "caption": "Lecture 18 · p3"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 18 · p3", "main": "**Heads and layers work like visiting a series of classes to improve yourself, one class after another.** In each class, every student gives you their own opinion on how to improve. Each student is a **head**.", "sub": ["You then **combine** all of those opinions into one improved version of yourself, and combining the heads' opinions is what a layer does.", "Then you go to the next class and do the same thing again, so **the classes are the layers**.", "Layers give an LLM **depth**, which is one round of improvement after another.", "Heads give an LLM **width**, which is many experts asked at the same time within each round."], "end": "", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture18/003.webp", "caption": "Lecture 18 · p3"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 18 · p3", "main": "**Each head is a set of weights, and every head in every layer learns.** An expert's knowledge is in its weights. Every head learns how to improve the vectors so that the last layer can predict the next word.", "sub": ["Large systems have **a hundred or more layers** and about **a hundred heads** in each layer. They need that many because language, vision and the other tasks are very complicated.", "Training takes a sentence such as \"the dog that ... barks\" from text, and the system predicts the next word.", "If the prediction is wrong, **every head in every layer** adjusts its weights with backpropagation, so the next prediction is closer to barks.", "Training at this size uses enormous resources, and in some states a large share of the water goes to the data centers that run it."], "end": "", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture18/003.webp", "caption": "Lecture 18 · p3"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 18 · p4", "main": "**A dynamical system is a rule that is fed its own output again and again.** Numbers go in, the rule acts on them, and numbers come out. The output is then fed back in as the next input.", "sub": ["In the **harmonic oscillator**, the two numbers are the position x and the velocity v.", "If x starts at one and v starts at zero, the first step leaves x the same and makes v go down.", "x and v **take turns**. The new x is calculated from the old v, and then the new v is calculated from the new x.", "Step by step, the rule draws a **cosine** for x."], "end": "Fields as different as finance use the same idea with other rules. Stock prices are modeled with equations that feed back on themselves, although the real market never follows them exactly.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture18/004.webp", "caption": "Lecture 18 · p4"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 18 · p4", "main": "**Jordan's network combined neural networks with dynamical systems, because speech production changes over time.** The network's **output is copied back into its state units**. The state units also keep a fading copy of their own previous values.", "sub": ["**Plan units** and **state units** feed the hidden layer, and the hidden layer feeds the output.", "The output then loops back into the state units, and the same weights are used at every step.", "After more than **thirty years of improvements**, this idea became the **LLM**."], "end": "", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture18/004.webp", "caption": "Lecture 18 · p4"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 17 · p4 · Lecture 18 · p4", "main": "**An LLM is a dynamical system, which is why the Nobel Prize for AI was given in physics.** A mass-spring system starts from **initial conditions**, which are its starting position and speed, and the rule decides what happens next. An LLM's initial condition is its set of input vectors.", "sub": ["In an LLM, **each layer is the rule**, because each layer applies its weights to every vector and the next layer works on the result.", "So **the depth of the layers plays the role of time**. The vectors become **better vectors** step after step, until the last layer can predict the next word.", "If you follow one number up through the layers, it traces a curve similar to the oscillator's curve, though not an exact cosine.", "The calculation at every step is the **inner product**, repeated billions or trillions of times. Thousands of numbers are improved layer by layer, instead of two numbers changing over time."], "end": "So an LLM is a recurrent network laid out layer by layer. The spring's motion was laid out step by step in the same way when it was calculated on a computer.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture17/004.webp", "caption": "Lecture 17 · p4"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 18 · p4", "main": "**The rule in each layer of an LLM is the weights of its heads, and those weights are learned by predicting the next word.** Backpropagation sets those weights during training. Agentic systems also contain loops, such as the loop between the critic and the revision LLM.", "sub": ["So dynamical systems appear at every level of AI.", "The math of inner products that LLMs rely on was first studied deeply in **quantum mechanics**, about a hundred years ago."], "end": "So physics and AI are closely connected in more than one way.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture18/004.webp", "caption": "Lecture 18 · p4"}}], "questions": [{"source": "Lecture 18", "q": "What are heads, and how do they relate to layers?", "a": "A head is one expert, a set of weights that gives its own opinion on how to improve the vectors. Heads sit side by side inside each layer, and the layer combines all of their opinions into improved vectors. Layers go one after another from the input to the output, so each layer improves the vectors again."}, {"source": "Predicted", "q": "In the classroom of experts, what are the classes and what are the students?", "a": "The classes are the layers, because you visit them one after another. The students in each class are the heads, because each one gives a separate opinion at the same time. You combine those opinions before moving on to the next class."}, {"source": "Predicted", "q": "Which gives an LLM depth and which gives it width, heads or layers?", "a": "Layers give an LLM depth, because they are rounds of improvement that come one after another. Heads give it width, because many of them work side by side inside the same layer."}, {"source": "Predicted", "q": "An LLM has 5 layers with 4 heads in each layer. How many heads does it have, and which of them work at the same time?", "a": "It has 20 heads, because each of the 5 layers has 4. The 4 heads inside one layer work side by side at the same time. The layers come one after another, so layer 2 works on the vectors that layer 1 improved."}, {"source": "Predicted", "q": "The LLM predicts bark instead of barks after \"the dog that my parents ... \". Which weights change?", "a": "The weights of every head in every layer change. Backpropagation assigns each weight its share of the blame for the wrong prediction. Each weight is then adjusted a little, so the next prediction is closer to barks."}, {"source": "Predicted", "q": "Where is the knowledge of an LLM stored?", "a": "The knowledge of an LLM is stored in its weights. Each head is a set of weights, so the knowledge is spread across all the heads in all the layers. The vowel network's knowledge is stored in its weights in the same way."}, {"source": "Predicted", "q": "Why is an LLM called a dynamical system?", "a": "An LLM is called a dynamical system because it works by feeding a rule its own output again and again. A dynamical system is a rule fed its own output repeatedly, starting from initial conditions. In an LLM the input vectors are the initial conditions, and each layer applies its weights to turn them into better vectors. So the depth of the layers plays the role of time."}, {"source": "Predicted", "q": "In the harmonic oscillator, which two numbers update, and in what order?", "a": "Position x and velocity v update, and they take turns. First the new x is calculated from the old v, and then the new v is calculated from the new x. Repeating this step by step makes x trace out a cosine."}, {"source": "", "q": "How are heads different from layers?", "a": "**Heads work side by side inside one layer, and layers work one after another.** Each layer combines the results of its heads into improved vectors. The next layer then improves those vectors again."}, {"source": "", "q": "Why is a head not the same as an agent with a checking job?", "a": "**A head is a part inside one LLM, but an agent is a whole LLM with a task in a larger system.** A critic agent, for example, checks a draft. So heads and agents belong to different levels."}, {"source": "", "q": "What does the depth of an LLM share with a dynamical system changing over time?", "a": "**In both, the output of one step becomes the input to the next.** The state of the oscillator changes from moment to moment. The vectors of the LLM change from layer to layer."}, {"source": "", "q": "Does the dynamical-system comparison mean every layer has the same weights?", "a": "**No, each layer has its own learned weights.** The comparison is about a series of changing states. It does not need one identical rule at every layer."}]}, {"id": "agents-software", "title": "Agentic systems and Software 3.0", "lectures": "Lectures 17 and 18", "blocks": [{"ref": "Lecture 17 · p4 · Lecture 18 · p2", "main": "**An agentic system is a team of LLMs connected to each other, and an agent in AI today is just an LLM.** The LLMs talk to each other in English. So one LLM's output becomes another LLM's input.", "sub": ["**Neurons make neural networks, neural networks make LLMs, and LLMs make agentic systems**, the same way molecules make cells and cells make organs.", "Designing a group of LLMs to solve a problem is a real job today, and it needs problem solving more than knowledge of programming languages."], "end": "", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture17/004.webp", "caption": "Lecture 17 · p4"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 17 · p4 · Lecture 18 · p2", "main": "**In one agentic system, each LLM has its own task, and the draft passes from one LLM to the next.** An **outline LLM** receives the prompt and only makes an outline of the answer. A **writing LLM** turns the outline into full sentences.", "sub": ["A **critic LLM** checks the draft for problems, such as whether each statement follows from the one before.", "A **revision LLM** works back and forth with the critic. It decides whether the draft is good enough or sends it back, usually for only a few rounds.", "A **final LLM** produces the answer the user sees.", "Sometimes a **controller** agent passes the work from one LLM to the next."], "end": "The critic greatly reduced **hallucination**, which is when the system makes things up. The conversation between the LLMs is why many systems take a while to respond.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture17/004.webp", "caption": "Lecture 17 · p4"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 18 · p2", "main": "**The agents in an agentic system usually stop learning once they are trained, and they share a common memory.** Once each LLM is trained, its weights usually **do not change** while the agents work together. Some new systems are starting to learn their own connections, so they are partly self-designing.", "sub": ["The agents also know **computer languages**, such as Python and database languages, because they were trained to predict whole programs too.", "Agents share a **common memory**, where they can write and read information.", "The shared memory gives them a lot of their power, and it is also a danger, because it lets them coordinate in ways nobody planned."], "end": "", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture18/002.webp", "caption": "Lecture 18 · p2"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 17 · p4 · Lecture 18 · p2", "main": "**The connections between LLMs can form paths that nobody planned.** These paths let a system call other LLMs and do things that were not part of the design. Some of those things can be harmful.", "sub": ["Limiting those connections needs creative people from many fields.", "The same tools can also do good work. One agentic system acts as a patient teacher for a Native American language that is nearly lost.", "That teacher works even though there is far too little text in that language to train an ordinary LLM."], "end": "", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture17/004.webp", "caption": "Lecture 17 · p4"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 17 · p8 · Lecture 18 · p8", "main": "**An LLM that learns English syllables can be built by describing it in plain English to an agentic system.** Writing the program by hand would take months. The agentic system builds the LLM, trains it, and shows what it has learned along the way.", "sub": ["The request specified **five layers**, **four heads in each layer**, and **input vectors that are 64 numbers long**.", "It asked the system to pick **1000 English words at random** and train on them. Training meant taking each letter, trying to **predict the next letter**, and improving the weights.", "It also asked for **ten generated words after each step of training**, so each step reveals what the LLM knows about English at that point.", "So its knowledge can be compared with the syllable knowledge that speakers already have."], "end": "", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture17/008.webp", "caption": "Lecture 17 · p8"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 18 · p8", "main": "**The agentic system translated the request into its own technical terms and built a small character-level transformer.** A **character-level transformer** is an LLM that works one letter at a time, with **attention heads**. **Attention** means a head looks over the sequence to decide which earlier items matter.", "sub": ["Predicting barks requires paying attention to dog in the same way.", "The system chose to train on **pronunciations** instead of spellings, so the silent e in shave would not mislead it.", "The system also wrote the program in Python on its own."], "end": "", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture18/008.webp", "caption": "Lecture 18 · p8"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 18 · p8", "main": "**The results show how hard the syllable problem is, even for an LLM.** After training on 1000 words, the generated words were **still mostly gibberish**. Perhaps a few vowels were starting to appear in the right places.", "sub": ["The suggested next step was to train on **10,000 words** for many more steps."], "end": "So deciding which sounds can follow which seems easy for any speaker, but it is not easy even for an LLM.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture18/008.webp", "caption": "Lecture 18 · p8"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 18 · p8", "main": "**Software 1.0, 2.0 and 3.0 are three ways to build a program.** **Software 1.0** is writing a program in a programming language such as Python or C. **Software 2.0** is giving a neural network input data and output data, so the program becomes the network's **weights**.", "sub": ["**Software 3.0** is using an agentic system, a set of LLMs talking to each other and to the user, to build and run a program.", "Building a program this way with plain English instructions is called **vibe coding**.", "So someone with a good idea no longer needs to hire a programmer to build it."], "end": "", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture18/008.webp", "caption": "Lecture 18 · p8"}}], "questions": [{"source": "Lab 9", "q": "What is an agentic system, in terms of LLMs? Describe the example system.", "a": "An agentic system is a graph of LLMs, each with its own job, connected so that one LLM's output becomes the next one's input. In the example, one LLM outlines, one writes, one critiques, one revises and one produces the final version. The critic and the reviser form a loop, so the draft keeps going back until the critic is satisfied or the limit is reached."}, {"source": "Lab 9", "q": "What does the critic loop do, and what does it prevent that was common in earlier LLMs?", "a": "The critic loop sends the draft back until the critic is satisfied, so errors are caught and fixed before they are presented. It prevents hallucination, which was a common problem in earlier LLMs."}, {"source": "Predicted", "q": "What would happen if the critic LLM were removed?", "a": "Without the critic, the draft would go straight from writing to the final answer without being checked. So more made-up statements would reach the user, because the critic loop is what greatly reduced hallucination."}, {"source": "Predicted", "q": "What is the difference between Software 2.0 and Software 3.0?", "a": "In Software 2.0, a neural network is trained on input and output data, so the program is its weights. In Software 3.0, an agentic system builds and runs the program from plain English instructions, as in vibe coding."}, {"source": "", "q": "What is the difference between Software 1.0, 2.0 and 3.0?", "a": "**They differ in how the program gets its behavior.** In 1.0, a person writes the steps. In 2.0, a network learns its weights from examples. In 3.0, communicating LLMs build the program from plain English instructions."}, {"source": "", "q": "Why can Software 1.0, 2.0 and 3.0 work together?", "a": "**An agentic system uses trained networks, and those networks run as written programs.** So all three kinds of software can be part of one system. A newer kind does not have to replace an older one."}]}, {"id": "phonotactics", "title": "Phonotactics and guessing the next letter", "lectures": "Lectures 17 and 18", "blocks": [{"ref": "Lecture 17 · p8 · Lecture 18 · p6", "main": "**Speakers know a small system of sounds that decides how vowels and consonants can follow each other.** English has about 30,000 words, so training an LLM on whole words takes enormous data, money and energy. The sound system has about 26 letters, or **only a few dozen sounds**.", "sub": ["So a small LLM, with thousands of weights instead of trillions, can learn the syllable knowledge every speaker already has.", "A model that small can be studied closely."], "end": "", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture17/008.webp", "caption": "Lecture 17 · p8"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 17 · p6 · Lecture 18 · p6", "main": "**English allows only certain sequences of sounds in its words, and every speaker knows which ones are allowed.** **A simple English syllable is a consonant, then a vowel, then a consonant**, as in Don, mat, sit, cup, dog, fish and shave. The pattern is about **sounds, not letters**.", "sub": ["The silent e in shave, kite and rope does not count, because it is not pronounced.", "Two letters can spell one consonant sound, as in ship, chin and bath, so those words also fit the pattern."], "end": "", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture17/006.webp", "caption": "Lecture 17 · p6"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 17 · p6 · Lecture 18 · p6", "main": "**Syllables can also have clusters, and only some clusters are allowed.** A **cluster** is a group of consonants together. A cluster before the vowel is an **onset**, as in split, flat, black and glad. A cluster after the vowel is a **coda**, as in desk, help, milk and lamp.", "sub": ["Each real word has an impossible twin marked with a star, such as **flat** and **lfat**, **help** and **hepl**, and **lamp** and **lapm**.", "Many twins use the same letters in a different order, so the order of the sounds matters.", "**Sblit** and **desb** are impossible too, so a product called \"desb\" or \"lapm\" sounds un-English right away."], "end": "", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture17/006.webp", "caption": "Lecture 17 · p6"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 17 · p6", "main": "**The order of consonants on one side of the vowel is a mirror image of the order on the other side.** In flat, the l is right next to the vowel and the f is farther out. In help, the l is again right next to the vowel, now on the other side, and the p is farther out.", "sub": ["Each impossible twin puts the wrong consonant next to the vowel, so lfat and hepl break the mirror order."], "end": "", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture17/006.webp", "caption": "Lecture 17 · p6"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 17 · p6 · Lecture 18 · p6", "main": "**A long word is a chain of syllables, and each syllable follows the same rules.** Examples are ba-na-na, At-lan-tic and ath-lete. The sounds t and l are fine across a syllable break, but **tl cannot start an English syllable**.", "sub": ["That is why **Atlantic** is a word but **tlantic** is impossible.", "The sound **ng** can end a syllable, as in ring, but it can never start one."], "end": "So whether a sequence of sounds is allowed depends on where the syllable breaks are.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture17/006.webp", "caption": "Lecture 17 · p6"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 17 · p6 · Lecture 18 · p6", "main": "**Every language has its own sequence rules, and speakers learn them without being taught.** **Mandarin** allows only nasal sounds at the end of a syllable. So words like mat, sit and shave are impossible in Mandarin.", "sub": ["**Cantonese** also allows a final p, as in cup.", "**Russian and Polish** allow clusters like zb or dl at the start of a word, which English never allows.", "**Spanish** does not allow a word to start with s and t together. So Spanish speakers add an e at the start of a word like stadium, which keeps each syllable simple."], "end": "", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture17/006.webp", "caption": "Lecture 17 · p6"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 17 · p7 · Lecture 18 · p7", "main": "**Guessing the next letter of an English word shows what a speaker knows about sounds.** Each item is the start of an English word, and the task is to guess the next letter. People agree on their guesses quickly, and how much they agree changes from item to item.", "sub": ["**After a vowel, almost any letter can come next.**", "After \"ra,\" likely letters include t, n, r and l, and many other letters are possible too."], "end": "", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture17/007.webp", "caption": "Lecture 17 · p7"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 17 · p7 · Lecture 18 · p7", "main": "**At the start of a word, the choices narrow to exactly what an English onset allows.** Each beginning allows its own small set of next letters. Some letters can never come next.", "sub": ["After **sp**, the next letter is l, r or a vowel, and never n. After **k**, a vowel or n can come next, as in knee, and never t.", "After **b** and after **f**, the next letter is r, l or a vowel, and never n. After **sh**, a vowel or r can come next, as in shrimp.", "After **skr**, or after **m** at the start of a word, only a vowel can come.", "After **bram**, the letter b is almost certain, as in bramble, though a new syllable could also start there."], "end": "", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture17/007.webp", "caption": "Lecture 17 · p7"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 17 · p7 · Lecture 18 · p7", "main": "**Guessing the next letter is predicting the 16th vector, which is the same game an LLM plays.** A GPT trained only on this game learns English sounds and spelling. It learns them because its predictions can only improve by matching the patterns of English syllables.", "sub": ["The GPT's prediction is a **probability distribution** over the next letter, and the shape of that distribution holds the knowledge.", "After \"ra,\" the distribution is **flat**, spread over many letters, because English allows many choices after a vowel.", "After \"skr,\" the distribution is **sharply peaked** on a few vowels, because English allows almost nothing else there."], "end": "So the more the letters so far limit what can come next, the more the probability piles onto a few letters.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture17/007.webp", "caption": "Lecture 17 · p7"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 18 · p8", "main": "**The first attempt at producing English letter sequences was a 1948 paper by Claude Shannon.** The paper is \"A Mathematical Theory of Communication.\" Shannon worked by hand and produced letter sequences that came closer and closer to English. His sequences went from random strings of consonants, to strings with vowels, to strings with real short words.", "sub": ["That paper started **information theory**, one of the most important fields in engineering, and information theory is still important for LLMs today.", "**Viterbi**, one of the developers of dynamic programming, published his most famous work in an information theory journal.", "So the ideas behind backpropagation and LLMs share the same roots."], "end": "", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture18/008.webp", "caption": "Lecture 18 · p8"}}], "questions": [{"source": "Lab 9", "q": "Which could be English words: blick, bnick, sblat, splat, dlag, lisk, lisb, lapm, lamp? For each one that could not, name the problem.", "a": "Blick, splat, lisk and lamp could be English words. Bnick, sblat and dlag are impossible because bn, sb and dl cannot start a syllable. Lisb and lapm are impossible because sb and pm cannot end a syllable."}, {"source": "Lab 9", "q": "Each of these starts an English word: pl_, st_ and th_. What could come next, and what could not?", "a": "After pl only a vowel can come, as in play, so a consonant such as n cannot. After st a vowel or r can come, as in star and street, but l and k cannot. After th, r, w or a vowel can come, as in three, thwart and thin, but l and n cannot."}, {"source": "Predicted", "q": "After which start is the next-letter distribution flatter, ra_ or skr_? Why?", "a": "The distribution is flatter after ra. After a vowel almost any letter can come next, so the probability is spread over many letters. After skr only a vowel can come, so the probability piles onto a few vowels and the distribution is sharply peaked."}, {"source": "Predicted", "q": "Why is Atlantic an English word but tlantic impossible?", "a": "Atlantic is a possible word because its t and l are on opposite sides of a syllable break, so each syllable follows the rules. In tlantic, tl would have to start a syllable, and English does not allow tl at the start of a syllable."}, {"source": "Predicted", "q": "Could ngop be an English word? Could flim?", "a": "Ngop could not be an English word, but flim could. The sound ng can end a syllable, as in ring, but it can never start one. Flim is possible because fl is an allowed onset, as in flat, and im is an allowed ending."}, {"source": "Predicted", "q": "Why does a GPT trained only to guess the next letter end up knowing English syllable structure?", "a": "A GPT ends up knowing syllable structure because the patterns of English syllables decide which letters can come next. Its predictions can only improve by matching those patterns. So the knowledge ends up in the shape of its probability distributions, which are flat after a vowel and peaked after a start such as skr."}, {"source": "Predicted", "q": "Which letter cannot come next after sp_ at the start of a word: l, r, n or a?", "a": "N cannot come next. After sp only l, r or a vowel can come, as in split, spring and spa, because spn is not an allowed English onset."}, {"source": "Predicted", "q": "Which letter cannot come next after k_ at the start of a word: a, n or t?", "a": "T cannot come next. After k a vowel or n can come, as in knee, but kt cannot start an English syllable."}, {"source": "Predicted", "q": "What can come next after b_ or f_ at the start of a word, and what cannot?", "a": "After b or f, only r, l or a vowel can come next, as in bring, black, frog and flat. N cannot come next, because bn and fn cannot start an English syllable."}, {"source": "Predicted", "q": "What can come next after sh_ at the start of a word?", "a": "After sh, only a vowel or r can come next, as in ship and shrimp. Shr is the only consonant cluster that English allows there."}, {"source": "Predicted", "q": "What can come next after skr_?", "a": "After skr, only a vowel can come next, as in scream and scrap. Skr already fills the onset, so no other consonant can be added."}, {"source": "Predicted", "q": "Why are lfat, hepl and lapm impossible when flat, help and lamp are words?", "a": "Lfat, hepl and lapm are impossible because the order of a cluster is a mirror image around the vowel. In flat and help the l sits right next to the vowel, and in lamp the m does. Each impossible twin puts the wrong consonant next to the vowel, so it breaks the mirror order."}, {"source": "Predicted", "q": "Is ship a consonant, vowel, consonant word even though it has four letters?", "a": "Yes, ship is a consonant, vowel, consonant word. The pattern is about sounds, not letters, and sh is one sound. So ship is the consonant sh, the vowel i and the consonant p."}, {"source": "", "q": "How do the onset and the coda limit which English sound sequences are possible?", "a": "**Each position in the syllable allows only certain combinations.** The onset comes before the vowel, and the coda comes after it. So whether a sequence is allowed depends on where it sits in the syllable."}, {"source": "", "q": "Why is Atlantic a word but tlantic impossible?", "a": "**In Atlantic, the t ends one syllable and the l starts the next.** In tlantic, tl would have to start a syllable as one onset. English does not allow tl as an onset, so tlantic is impossible."}, {"source": "", "q": "What can guessing the next letter show about word beginnings?", "a": "**It shows which continuations English allows at the start of a word.** A beginning limits what can come next. After sp, only l, r or a vowel fits, so a good guess reflects those learned limits."}, {"source": "", "q": "Why is the next-letter distribution spread out after some beginnings and peaked after others?", "a": "**Some beginnings allow many next letters, and others allow very few.** After ra, almost any letter can follow, so the distribution is flat. After skr, only a vowel can follow, so it is sharply peaked."}]}]};
